1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:05,280
We talked three years ago. In your view, what has 
been the biggest update over the last three years? 

2
00:00:05,280 --> 00:00:08,480
What has been the biggest difference 
between what it felt like then versus now? 

3
00:00:10,880 --> 00:00:18,800
Broadly speaking, the exponential of the 
underlying technology has gone about as  

4
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:23,520
I expected it to go.
There's plus or minus  

5
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,440
a year or two here and there.
I don't know that I would've  

6
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:34,320
predicted the specific direction of code.
But when I look at the exponential,  

7
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:39,600
it is roughly what I expected in terms of 
the march of the models from smart high  

8
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:44,640
school student to smart college student to 
beginning to do PhD and professional stuff,  

9
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:49,600
and in the case of code reaching beyond that.
The frontier is a little bit uneven, but it's  

10
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:55,200
roughly what I expected.
What has been the most surprising  

11
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:02,000
thing is the lack of public recognition of how 
close we are to the end of the exponential. 

12
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,040
To me, it is absolutely wild that you have 
people — within the bubble and outside the  

13
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:19,360
bubble — talking about the same tired, old 
hot-button political issues, when we are  

14
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:24,000
near the end of the exponential.
I want to understand what that  

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,040
exponential looks like right now.
The first question I asked you when  

16
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:30,800
we recorded three years ago was, "what’s 
up with scaling and why does it work?" 

17
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,640
I have a similar question now, 
but it feels more complicated. 

18
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:41,840
At least from the public's point of view, three 
years ago there were well-known public trends  

19
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:45,120
across many orders of magnitude of compute 
where you could see how the loss improves. 

20
00:01:45,120 --> 00:01:49,680
Now we have RL scaling and there's 
no publicly known scaling law for it. 

21
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:54,080
It's not even clear what the story is.
Is this supposed to be teaching the model skills? 

22
00:01:54,080 --> 00:01:59,040
Is it supposed to be teaching meta-learning?
What is the scaling hypothesis at this point? 

23
00:01:59,840 --> 00:02:06,480
I actually have the same hypothesis 
I had even all the way back in 2017. 

24
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:11,760
I think I talked about it last time, but I wrote 
a doc called "The Big Blob of Compute Hypothesis". 

25
00:02:12,880 --> 00:02:15,600
It wasn't about the scaling of 
language models in particular. 

26
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:21,680
When I wrote it GPT-1 had just come out.
That was one among many things. 

27
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:26,160
Back in those days there was robotics.
People tried to work on reasoning as  

28
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:30,240
a separate thing from language models, 
and there was scaling of the kind of RL  

29
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:42,160
that happened in AlphaGo and in Dota at OpenAI.
People remember StarCraft at DeepMind, AlphaStar. 

30
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:52,160
It was written as a more general document.
Rich Sutton put out "The Bitter  

31
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:57,520
Lesson" a couple years later.
The hypothesis is basically the same. 

32
00:02:57,520 --> 00:03:04,800
What it says is that all the cleverness, all the 
techniques, all the "we need a new method to do  

33
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:08,560
something", that doesn't matter very much.
There are only a few things that matter. 

34
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:13,680
I think I listed seven of them.
One is how much raw compute you have. 

35
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:20,320
The second is the quantity of data.
The third is the quality and distribution of data. 

36
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:26,560
It needs to be a broad distribution.
The fourth is how long you train for. 

37
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:32,080
The fifth is that you need an objective 
function that can scale to the moon. 

38
00:03:32,080 --> 00:03:35,840
The pre-training objective function 
is one such objective function. 

39
00:03:36,720 --> 00:03:42,640
Another is the RL objective 
function that says you have a goal,  

40
00:03:42,640 --> 00:03:48,160
you're going to go out and reach the goal.
Within that, there's objective rewards like  

41
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:52,160
you see in math and coding, and there's 
more subjective rewards like you see in  

42
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:59,200
RLHF or higher-order versions of that.
Then the sixth and seventh were things  

43
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:04,720
around normalization or conditioning, 
just getting the numerical stability  

44
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:10,880
so that the big blob of compute flows in this 
laminar way instead of running into problems. 

45
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:15,680
That was the hypothesis, and 
it's a hypothesis I still hold. 

46
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:19,600
I don't think I've seen very 
much that is not in line with it. 

47
00:04:21,040 --> 00:04:29,120
The pre-training scaling laws were one example 
of what we see there. Those have continued going.  

48
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:35,920
Now it's been widely reported, 
we feel good about pre-training. 

49
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:41,920
It’s continuing to give us gains.
What has changed is that now we're  

50
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:46,160
also seeing the same thing for RL.
We're seeing a pre-training phase  

51
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:54,080
and then an RL phase on top of that.
With RL, it’s actually just the same. 

52
00:04:55,280 --> 00:05:05,280
Even other companies have published things in 
some of their releases that say, "We train the  

53
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:14,640
model on math contests — AIME or other things 
— and how well the model does is log-linear in  

54
00:05:14,640 --> 00:05:17,520
how long we've trained it."
We see that as well,  

55
00:05:17,520 --> 00:05:21,840
and it's not just math contests.
It's a wide variety of RL tasks. 

56
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:27,280
We're seeing the same scaling in 
RL that we saw for pre-training. 

57
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:31,840
You mentioned Rich Sutton and "The Bitter Lesson".
I interviewed him last year,  

58
00:05:31,840 --> 00:05:38,560
and he's actually very non-LLM-pilled.
I don’t know if this is his perspective,  

59
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:44,800
but one way to paraphrase his objection is: 
Something which possesses the true core of human  

60
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:51,040
learning would not require all these billions 
of dollars of data and compute and these bespoke  

61
00:05:51,040 --> 00:05:57,920
environments, to learn how to use Excel, how to 
use PowerPoint, how to navigate a web browser. 

62
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:04,400
The fact that we have to build in these skills 
using these RL environments hints that we are  

63
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:13,040
actually lacking a core human learning algorithm.
So we're scaling the wrong thing. That does raise  

64
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:16,640
the question. Why are we doing all this RL scaling 
if we think there's something that's going to be  

65
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:23,600
human-like in its ability to learn on the fly?
I think this puts together several things that  

66
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:29,120
should be thought of differently.
There is a genuine puzzle here,  

67
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:33,040
but it may not matter.
In fact, I would guess it probably  

68
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:37,440
doesn't matter. There is an interesting thing. Let 
me take the RL out of it for a second, because I  

69
00:06:37,440 --> 00:06:42,960
actually think it's a red herring to say that RL 
is any different from pre-training in this matter. 

70
00:06:43,760 --> 00:06:47,760
If we look at pre-training 
scaling, it was very interesting  

71
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:59,120
back in 2017 when Alec Radford was doing GPT-1.
The models before GPT-1 were trained on datasets  

72
00:06:59,120 --> 00:07:08,080
that didn't represent a wide distribution of text.
You had very standard language  

73
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:11,360
modeling benchmarks.
GPT-1 itself was trained on  

74
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:17,840
a bunch of fanfiction, I think actually.
It was literary text, which is a very  

75
00:07:17,840 --> 00:07:23,280
small fraction of the text you can get.
In those days it was like a billion words  

76
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:32,880
or something, so small datasets representing 
a pretty narrow distribution of what you can  

77
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:43,280
see in the world. It didn't generalize well. 
If you did better on some fanfiction corpus,  

78
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:47,360
it wouldn't generalize that well to other 
tasks. We had all these measures. We had  

79
00:07:47,360 --> 00:07:53,840
all these measures of how well it did at 
predicting all these other kinds of texts. 

80
00:07:55,840 --> 00:08:01,520
It was only when you trained over all the tasks 
on the internet — when you did a general internet  

81
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:06,960
scrape from something like Common Crawl or 
scraping links in Reddit, which is what we did for  

82
00:08:06,960 --> 00:08:15,840
GPT-2 — that you started to get generalization.
I think we're seeing the same thing on RL. 

83
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:23,120
We're starting first with simple RL tasks like 
training on math competitions, then moving to  

84
00:08:24,640 --> 00:08:31,760
broader training that involves things like code.
Now we're moving to many other tasks. 

85
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:35,360
I think then we're going to 
increasingly get generalization. 

86
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:39,920
So that kind of takes out the 
RL vs. pre-training side of it. 

87
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:49,600
But there is a puzzle either way, which is that 
in pre-training we use trillions of tokens. 

88
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:54,560
Humans don't see trillions of words.
So there is an actual sample  

89
00:08:54,560 --> 00:08:59,920
efficiency difference here.
There is actually something different here. 

90
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:06,400
The models start from scratch 
and they need much more training. 

91
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:15,600
But we also see that once they're trained, 
if we give them a long context length of  

92
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:17,280
a million — the only thing blocking long 
context is inference — they're very good at  

93
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:23,360
learning and adapting within that context.
So I don’t know the full answer to this. 

94
00:09:24,080 --> 00:09:28,320
I think there's something going 
on where pre-training is not like  

95
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:32,480
the process of humans learning, but it's 
somewhere between the process of humans  

96
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:38,960
learning and the process of human evolution.
We get many of our priors from evolution. 

97
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:43,200
Our brain isn't just a blank slate.
Whole books have been written about this. 

98
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:45,440
The language models are 
much more like blank slates. 

99
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:50,400
They literally start as random weights, whereas 
the human brain starts with all these regions  

100
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:56,480
connected to all these inputs and outputs.
Maybe we should think of pre-training — and  

101
00:09:56,480 --> 00:10:02,560
for that matter, RL as well — as something 
that exists in the middle space between  

102
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:10,800
human evolution and human on-the-spot learning.
And we should think of the in-context learning  

103
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:17,440
that the models do as something between long-term 
human learning and short-term human learning.  

104
00:10:17,440 --> 00:10:22,640
So there's this hierarchy. There’s evolution, 
there's long-term learning, there's short-term  

105
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:28,720
learning, and there's just human reaction.
The LLM phases exist along this spectrum,  

106
00:10:28,720 --> 00:10:34,960
but not necessarily at exactly the same points.
There’s no analog to some of the human modes  

107
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:40,000
of learning the LLMs are falling in 
between the points. Does that make sense? 

108
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:42,400
Yes, although some things 
are still a bit confusing. 

109
00:10:42,400 --> 00:10:45,840
For example, if the analogy is that this 
is like evolution so it's fine that it's  

110
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:51,520
not sample efficient, then if we're 
going to get super sample-efficient  

111
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:56,960
agent from in-context learning, why are we 
bothering to build all these RL environments? 

112
00:10:56,960 --> 00:11:00,400
There are companies whose work seems to 
be teaching models how to use this API,  

113
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:04,560
how to use Slack, how to use whatever.
It's confusing to me why there's so much emphasis  

114
00:11:04,560 --> 00:11:10,640
on that if the kind of agent that can just learn 
on the fly is emerging or has already emerged. 

115
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:17,120
I can't speak for the emphasis of anyone else.
I can only talk about how we think about it. 

116
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:25,120
The goal is not to teach the model 
every possible skill within RL,  

117
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:29,600
just as we don't do that within pre-training.
Within pre-training, we're not trying to expose  

118
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:35,600
the model to every possible way 
that words could be put together. 

119
00:11:37,280 --> 00:11:43,360
Rather, the model trains on a lot of things and 
then reaches generalization across pre-training. 

120
00:11:43,920 --> 00:11:53,440
That was the transition from GPT-1 to GPT-2 that 
I saw up close. The model reaches a point. I had  

121
00:11:53,440 --> 00:12:01,440
these moments where I was like, "Oh yeah, you 
just give the model a list of numbers — this is  

122
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:05,920
the cost of the house, this is the square feet of 
the house — and the model completes the pattern  

123
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:08,480
and does linear regression."
Not great, but it does it,  

124
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:16,000
and it's never seen that exact thing before.
So to the extent that we are building these  

125
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:24,720
RL environments, the goal is very similar to what 
was done five or ten years ago with pre-training. 

126
00:12:26,560 --> 00:12:32,240
We're trying to get a whole bunch of data, not 
because we want to cover a specific document or a  

127
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:39,760
specific skill, but because we want to generalize.
I think the framework you're laying down obviously  

128
00:12:39,760 --> 00:12:46,000
makes sense. We're making progress toward AGI. 
Nobody at this point disagrees we're going to  

129
00:12:46,000 --> 00:12:49,520
achieve AGI this century.
The crux is you say we're  

130
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:54,320
hitting the end of the exponential.
Somebody else looks at this and says,  

131
00:12:55,920 --> 00:13:00,080
"We've been making progress since 2012, 
and by 2035 we'll have a human-like agent." 

132
00:13:04,800 --> 00:13:07,360
Obviously we’re seeing in these models 
the kinds of things that evolution did,  

133
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:11,520
or that learning within a human lifetime does.
I want to understand what you’re seeing  

134
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:13,920
that makes you think it's one 
year away and not ten years away. 

135
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:25,040
There are two claims you could make 
here, one stronger and one weaker. 

136
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:34,400
Starting with the weaker claim, when 
I first saw the scaling back in 2019,  

137
00:13:35,120 --> 00:13:43,280
I wasn’t sure. This was a 50/50 thing. I 
thought I saw something. My claim was that this  

138
00:13:43,280 --> 00:13:50,800
was much more likely than anyone thinks.
Maybe there's a 50% chance this happens. 

139
00:13:51,600 --> 00:14:00,480
On the basic hypothesis of, as you put it, within 
ten years we'll get to what I call a "country of  

140
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:06,960
geniuses in a data center", I'm at 90% on that.
It's hard to go much higher than 90%  

141
00:14:06,960 --> 00:14:13,440
because the world is so unpredictable.
Maybe the irreducible uncertainty puts us at 95%,  

142
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:23,040
where you get to things like multiple companies 
having internal turmoil, Taiwan gets invaded,  

143
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:30,080
all the fabs get blown up by missiles.
Now you've jinxed us, Dario. 

144
00:14:30,080 --> 00:14:42,240
You could construct a 5% world where 
things get delayed for ten years. 

145
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:49,200
There's another 5% which is that I'm very 
confident on tasks that can be verified. 

146
00:14:50,160 --> 00:14:53,200
With coding, except for that 
irreducible uncertainty,  

147
00:14:54,400 --> 00:14:58,160
I think we'll be there in one or two years.
There's no way we will not be there in ten years  

148
00:14:58,160 --> 00:15:05,920
in terms of being able to do end-to-end coding.
My one little bit of fundamental uncertainty,  

149
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:12,160
even on long timescales, is about tasks that 
aren't verifiable: planning a mission to Mars;  

150
00:15:14,320 --> 00:15:21,440
doing some fundamental scientific 
discovery like CRISPR; writing a novel. 

151
00:15:21,440 --> 00:15:27,600
It’s hard to verify those tasks.
I am almost certain we have a  

152
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:33,120
reliable path to get there, but if there's 
a little bit of uncertainty it's there. 

153
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:39,600
On the ten-year timeline I'm at 90%, 
which is about as certain as you can be. 

154
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:46,240
I think it's crazy to say that 
this won't happen by 2035. 

155
00:15:46,240 --> 00:15:48,800
In some sane world, it would 
be outside the mainstream. 

156
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:58,720
But the emphasis on verification hints to me a 
lack of belief that these models are generalized. 

157
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:03,680
If you think about humans, we're both good 
at things for which we get verifiable reward  

158
00:16:03,680 --> 00:16:07,920
and things for which we don't.
No, this is why I’m almost sure. 

159
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:12,640
We already see substantial generalization 
from things that verify to things that  

160
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:15,440
don't. We're already seeing that.
But it seems like you were emphasizing  

161
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:20,880
this as a spectrum which will split apart 
which domains in which we see more progress. 

162
00:16:21,600 --> 00:16:27,600
That doesn't seem like how humans get better.
The world in which we don't get there is the world  

163
00:16:27,600 --> 00:16:34,800
in which we do all the verifiable things.
Many of them generalize,  

164
00:16:34,800 --> 00:16:40,080
but we don't fully get there.
We don’t fully color in the other side  

165
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:47,520
of the box. It's not a binary thing.
Even if generalization is weak and you can only do  

166
00:16:47,520 --> 00:16:49,680
verifiable domains, it's not clear to me you could 
automate software engineering in such a world. 

167
00:16:49,680 --> 00:16:58,480
You are "a software engineer" in some sense, but 
part of being a software engineer for you involves  

168
00:16:58,480 --> 00:17:03,520
writing long memos about your grand vision.
I don’t think that’s part of the job of SWE. 

169
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:04,960
That's part of the job of the 
company, not SWE specifically. 

170
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:09,280
But SWE does involve design 
documents and other things like that. 

171
00:17:10,400 --> 00:17:12,960
The models are already pretty 
good at writing comments. 

172
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:24,480
Again, I’m making much weaker claims here than 
I believe, to distinguish between two things. 

173
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:26,640
We're already almost there 
for software engineering. 

174
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,680
By what metric? There's one metric which is 
how many lines of code are written by AI. 

175
00:17:32,800 --> 00:17:36,880
If you consider other productivity improvements 
in the history of software engineering,  

176
00:17:36,880 --> 00:17:40,960
compilers write all the lines of software.
There's a difference between how many lines  

177
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:46,560
are written and how big the productivity 
improvement is. "We’re almost there" meaning…  

178
00:17:47,120 --> 00:17:50,480
How big is the productivity improvement, 
not just how many lines are written by AI? 

179
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:57,120
I actually agree with you on this.
I've made a series of predictions on  

180
00:17:57,120 --> 00:18:02,000
code and software engineering.
I think people have repeatedly misunderstood them. 

181
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:09,520
Let me lay out the spectrum.
About eight or nine months ago,  

182
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:16,800
I said the AI model will be writing 90% of 
the lines of code in three to six months. 

183
00:18:16,800 --> 00:18:21,680
That happened, at least at some places.
It happened at Anthropic, happened with  

184
00:18:21,680 --> 00:18:27,520
many people downstream using our models.
But that's actually a very weak criterion. 

185
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:32,880
People thought I was saying that we won't need 90% 
of the software engineers. Those things are worlds  

186
00:18:32,880 --> 00:18:41,200
apart. The spectrum is: 90% of code is written by 
the model, 100% of code is written by the model. 

187
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:47,600
That's a big difference in productivity.
90% of the end-to-end SWE tasks — including  

188
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:54,160
things like compiling, setting up clusters 
and environments, testing features,  

189
00:18:54,160 --> 00:19:02,480
writing memos — are done by the models. 100% 
of today's SWE tasks are done by the models. 

190
00:19:02,480 --> 00:19:06,080
Even when that happens, it doesn't mean 
software engineers are out of a job. 

191
00:19:06,080 --> 00:19:10,240
There are new higher-level things 
they can do, where they can manage. 

192
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:15,760
Then further down the spectrum, there's 
90% less demand for SWEs, which I think  

193
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:21,440
will happen but this is a spectrum.
I wrote about it in "The Adolescence  

194
00:19:21,440 --> 00:19:25,040
of Technology" where I went through 
this kind of spectrum with farming. 

195
00:19:26,720 --> 00:19:29,120
I actually totally agree with you on that. 

196
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:32,160
These are very different 
benchmarks from each other,  

197
00:19:32,160 --> 00:19:37,760
but we're proceeding through them super fast.
Part of your vision is that going from 90 to 100  

198
00:19:38,667 --> 00:19:45,440
is going to happen fast, and that it 
leads to huge productivity improvements. 

199
00:19:45,440 --> 00:19:49,120
But what I notice is that even in greenfield 
projects people start with Claude Code or  

200
00:19:49,120 --> 00:19:54,640
something, people report starting a lot of 
projects… Do we see in the world out there  

201
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:58,240
a renaissance of software, all these new 
features that wouldn't exist otherwise? 

202
00:19:58,240 --> 00:20:02,080
At least so far, it doesn't seem like we see that.
So that does make me wonder. 

203
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:09,200
Even if I never had to intervene with 
Claude Code, the world is complicated.  

204
00:20:09,200 --> 00:20:14,640
Jobs are complicated. Closing the loop on 
self-contained systems, whether it’s just  

205
00:20:14,640 --> 00:20:19,760
writing software or something, how much 
broader gains would we see just from that? 

206
00:20:20,400 --> 00:20:24,640
Maybe that should dilute our estimation 
of the "country of geniuses". 

207
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:35,520
I simultaneously agree with you that it's a 
reason why these things don't happen instantly,  

208
00:20:35,520 --> 00:20:40,000
but at the same time, I think 
the effect is gonna be very fast. 

209
00:20:41,360 --> 00:20:47,040
You could have these two poles.
One is that AI is not going to make  

210
00:20:47,040 --> 00:20:52,480
progress. It's slow. It's going to take 
forever to diffuse within the economy. 

211
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:56,560
Economic diffusion has become one of 
these buzzwords that's a reason why  

212
00:20:56,560 --> 00:21:00,000
we're not going to make AI progress, 
or why AI progress doesn't matter. 

213
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:04,560
The other axis is that we'll get recursive 
self-improvement, the whole thing. 

214
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:07,760
Can't you just draw an 
exponential line on the curve? 

215
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:17,840
We're going to have Dyson spheres around the 
sun so many nanoseconds after we get recursive. 

216
00:21:17,840 --> 00:21:23,360
I'm completely caricaturing the view 
here, but there are these two extremes. 

217
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:30,800
But what we've seen from the beginning, at least 
if you look within Anthropic, there's this bizarre  

218
00:21:30,800 --> 00:21:38,040
10x per year growth in revenue that we've seen.
So in 2023, it was zero to $100 million. 

219
00:21:38,040 --> 00:21:45,600
In 2024, it was $100 million to $1 billion.
In 2025, it was $1 billion to $ 9-10 billion. 

220
00:21:46,320 --> 00:21:48,720
You guys should have just bought a billion 
dollars of your own products so you could just… 

221
00:21:50,640 --> 00:21:54,080
And the first month of this 
year, that exponential is... 

222
00:21:54,080 --> 00:22:04,080
You would think it would slow down, but we 
added another few billion to revenue in January. 

223
00:22:05,600 --> 00:22:10,240
Obviously that curve can't go on forever.
The GDP is only so large. 

224
00:22:10,240 --> 00:22:20,160
I would even guess that it bends somewhat this 
year, but that is a fast curve. That's a really  

225
00:22:20,160 --> 00:22:25,760
fast curve. I would bet it stays pretty fast 
even as the scale goes to the entire economy. 

226
00:22:25,760 --> 00:22:34,080
So I think we should be thinking about this middle 
world where things are extremely fast, but not  

227
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:39,840
instant, where they take time because of economic 
diffusion, because of the need to close the loop. 

228
00:22:39,840 --> 00:22:50,160
Because it's fiddly: "I have to do change 
management within my enterprise… I set this up,  

229
00:22:50,160 --> 00:22:54,880
but I have to change the security permissions 
on this in order to make it actually work…  

230
00:22:55,600 --> 00:23:01,520
I had this old piece of software that 
checks the model before it's compiled  

231
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:05,440
and released and I have to rewrite it.
Yes, the model can do that, but I have  

232
00:23:05,440 --> 00:23:09,200
to tell the model to do that.
It has to take time to do that." 

233
00:23:10,080 --> 00:23:17,600
So I think everything we've seen so far is 
compatible with the idea that there's one fast  

234
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:22,400
exponential that's the capability of the model.
Then there's another fast exponential  

235
00:23:22,400 --> 00:23:26,240
that's downstream of that, which is the 
diffusion of the model into the economy. 

236
00:23:26,240 --> 00:23:34,160
Not instant, not slow, much faster than any 
previous technology, but it has its limits. 

237
00:23:37,120 --> 00:23:43,440
When I look inside Anthropic, when I look at our 
customers: fast adoption, but not infinitely fast. 

238
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:45,520
Can I try a hot take on you?
Yeah. 

239
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:51,520
I feel like diffusion is cope that people say.
When the model isn't able to do something,  

240
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:56,960
they're like, "oh, but it's a diffusion issue."
But then you should use the comparison to humans. 

241
00:23:56,960 --> 00:24:01,760
You would think that the inherent advantages 
that AIs have would make diffusion a much easier  

242
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:06,080
