382
00:37:22,960 --> 00:37:25,120
Maybe you think that stops 
because you've won or whatever. 

383
00:37:25,120 --> 00:37:31,600
But why are we not seeing the person with 
the best coding model have this lasting  

384
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:36,400
advantage if in fact there are these enormous 
productivity gains from the last coding model. 

385
00:37:38,160 --> 00:37:45,040
I think my model of the situation is that 
there's an advantage that's gradually growing. 

386
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:51,040
I would say right now the coding 
models give maybe, I don't know,  

387
00:37:51,040 --> 00:38:01,280
a 15-20% total factor speed up. That's 
my view. Six months ago, it was maybe 5%. 

388
00:38:01,280 --> 00:38:05,680
So it didn't matter. 5% doesn't register.
It's now just getting to the point where it's  

389
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:11,680
one of several factors that kind of matters.
That's going to keep speeding up. 

390
00:38:12,400 --> 00:38:18,480
I think six months ago, there were several 
companies that were at roughly the same  

391
00:38:18,480 --> 00:38:25,920
point because this wasn't a notable factor, but 
I think it's starting to speed up more and more. 

392
00:38:25,920 --> 00:38:32,800
I would also say there are multiple companies that 
write models that are used for code and we're not  

393
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:40,400
perfectly good at preventing some of these other 
companies from using our models internally. 

394
00:38:41,120 --> 00:38:49,520
So I think everything we're seeing is 
consistent with this kind of snowball model. 

395
00:38:52,000 --> 00:39:00,880
Again, my theme in all of this is all of this 
is soft takeoff, soft, smooth exponentials,  

396
00:39:00,880 --> 00:39:05,760
although the exponentials are relatively steep.
So we're seeing this snowball gather momentum  

397
00:39:05,760 --> 00:39:13,120
where it's like 10%, 20%, 25%, 40%.
As you go, Amdahl's law, you have  

398
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:17,440
to get all the things that are preventing 
you from closing the loop out of the way. 

399
00:39:17,440 --> 00:39:20,960
But this is one of the biggest 
priorities within Anthropic. 

400
00:39:22,560 --> 00:39:29,440
Stepping back, before in the stack we were talking 
about when do we get this on-the-job learning? 

401
00:39:29,440 --> 00:39:32,880
It seems like the point you were making 
on the coding thing is that we actually  

402
00:39:32,880 --> 00:39:36,080
don't need on-the-job learning.
You can have tremendous productivity  

403
00:39:36,080 --> 00:39:40,400
improvements, you can have potentially trillions 
of dollars of revenue for AI companies, without  

404
00:39:40,400 --> 00:39:47,200
this basic human ability to learn on the job.
Maybe that's not your claim, you should clarify. 

405
00:39:47,200 --> 00:39:53,920
But in most domains of economic activity, people 
say, "I hired somebody, they weren't that useful  

406
00:39:53,920 --> 00:39:58,240
for the first few months, and then over time 
they built up the context, understanding." 

407
00:39:58,240 --> 00:40:00,160
It's actually hard to define 
what we're talking about here. 

408
00:40:00,160 --> 00:40:05,680
But they got something and then now they're 
a powerhorse and they're so valuable to us. 

409
00:40:05,680 --> 00:40:12,000
If AI doesn't develop this ability to learn on the 
fly, I'm a bit skeptical that we're going to see  

410
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:17,680
huge changes to the world without that ability.
I think two things here. There's the state  

411
00:40:17,680 --> 00:40:22,000
of the technology right now.
Again, we have these two stages. 

412
00:40:22,000 --> 00:40:27,760
We have the pre-training and RL stage where 
you throw a bunch of data and tasks into  

413
00:40:27,760 --> 00:40:31,600
the models and then they generalize.
So it's like learning, but it's like  

414
00:40:31,600 --> 00:40:38,480
learning from more data and not learning 
over one human or one model's lifetime. 

415
00:40:38,480 --> 00:40:42,560
So again, this is situated between 
evolution and human learning. 

416
00:40:42,560 --> 00:40:45,680
But once you learn all 
those skills, you have them. 

417
00:40:45,680 --> 00:40:52,640
Just like with pre-training, just how the models 
know more, if I look at a pre-trained model,  

418
00:40:52,640 --> 00:40:55,920
it knows more about the history 
of samurai in Japan than I do. 

419
00:40:55,920 --> 00:41:03,520
It knows more about baseball than I do.
It knows more about low-pass filters  

420
00:41:03,520 --> 00:41:08,960
and electronics, all of these things.
Its knowledge is way broader than mine. 

421
00:41:08,960 --> 00:41:18,160
So I think even just that may get us to the 
point where the models are better at everything. 

422
00:41:18,160 --> 00:41:24,000
We also have, again, just with scaling the kind 
of existing setup, the in-context learning. 

423
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:27,520
I would describe it as kind of 
like human on-the-job learning,  

424
00:41:27,520 --> 00:41:33,120
but a little weaker and a little short term.
You look at in-context learning and if you give  

425
00:41:33,120 --> 00:41:37,520
the model a bunch of examples it does get it.
There's real learning that happens in context. 

426
00:41:38,160 --> 00:41:42,160
A million tokens is a lot.
That can be days of human learning. 

427
00:41:42,880 --> 00:41:50,400
If you think about the model reading 
a million words, how long would it  

428
00:41:50,400 --> 00:41:57,200
take me to read a million? Days or weeks 
at least. So you have these two things. 

429
00:41:57,200 --> 00:42:01,600
I think these two things within the existing 
paradigm may just be enough to get you the  

430
00:42:01,600 --> 00:42:04,800
"country of geniuses in a data center".
I don't know for sure, but I think  

431
00:42:04,800 --> 00:42:10,800
they're going to get you a large fraction of it.
There may be gaps, but I certainly think that just  

432
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:17,600
as things are, this is enough to generate 
trillions of dollars of revenue. That's one. Two,  

433
00:42:17,600 --> 00:42:23,680
is this idea of continual learning, this 
idea of a single model learning on the job. 

434
00:42:24,320 --> 00:42:29,280
I think we're working on that too.
There's a good chance that in the next  

435
00:42:29,280 --> 00:42:36,800
year or two, we also solve that.
Again, I think you get most  

436
00:42:36,800 --> 00:42:45,280
of the way there without it.
The trillions of dollars a year market,  

437
00:42:45,280 --> 00:42:49,440
maybe all of the national security implications 
and the safety implications that I wrote about in  

438
00:42:49,440 --> 00:42:57,840
"Adolescence of Technology" can happen without it.
But we, and I imagine others, are working on it. 

439
00:42:57,840 --> 00:43:03,040
There's a good chance that we will 
get there within the next year or two. 

440
00:43:03,040 --> 00:43:06,320
There are a bunch of ideas.
I won't go into all of them in detail, but  

441
00:43:07,040 --> 00:43:10,880
one is just to make the context longer.
There's nothing preventing  

442
00:43:10,880 --> 00:43:14,400
longer contexts from working.
You just have to train at longer contexts  

443
00:43:14,400 --> 00:43:18,720
and then learn to serve them at inference.
Both of those are engineering problems that  

444
00:43:18,720 --> 00:43:21,600
we are working on and I would assume 
others are working on them as well. 

445
00:43:22,480 --> 00:43:26,080
This context length increase, it seemed 
like there was a period from 2020 to 2023  

446
00:43:26,080 --> 00:43:31,520
where from GPT-3 to GPT-4 Turbo, there was an 
increase from 2000 context lengths to 128K. 

447
00:43:31,520 --> 00:43:37,040
I feel like for the two-ish years since 
then, we've been in the same-ish ballpark. 

448
00:43:37,040 --> 00:43:41,440
When context lengths get much longer 
than that, people report qualitative  

449
00:43:41,440 --> 00:43:45,600
degradation in the ability of the 
model to consider that full context. 

450
00:43:47,120 --> 00:43:50,640
So I'm curious what you're internally seeing 
that makes you think, "10 million contexts,  

451
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:54,480
100 million contexts to get six months 
of human learning and building context". 

452
00:43:54,480 --> 00:43:58,480
This isn't a research problem. This is 
an engineering and inference problem. 

453
00:43:58,480 --> 00:44:03,280
If you want to serve long context, you 
have to store your entire KV cache. 

454
00:44:06,560 --> 00:44:11,600
It's difficult to store all the memory 
in the GPUs, to juggle the memory around. 

455
00:44:11,600 --> 00:44:15,280
I don't even know the details.
At this point, this is at a level of detail  

456
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:21,680
that I'm no longer able to follow, although I 
knew it in the GPT-3 era. "These are the weights,  

457
00:44:21,680 --> 00:44:26,880
these are the activations you have to store…"
But these days the whole thing is flipped  

458
00:44:26,880 --> 00:44:34,000
because we have MoE models and all of that.
Regarding this degradation you're talking about,  

459
00:44:34,640 --> 00:44:41,200
without getting too specific, there's two things.
There's the context length you train at and  

460
00:44:41,200 --> 00:44:45,440
there's a context length that you serve at.
If you train at a small context length  

461
00:44:45,440 --> 00:44:49,520
and then try to serve at a long context 
length, maybe you get these degradations. 

462
00:44:49,520 --> 00:44:52,880
It's better than nothing, you might still 
offer it, but you get these degradations. 

463
00:44:52,880 --> 00:44:55,040
Maybe it's harder to train 
at a long context length. 

464
00:44:56,880 --> 00:45:01,040
I want to, at the same time, ask 
about maybe some rabbit holes. 

465
00:45:01,600 --> 00:45:04,560
Wouldn't you expect that if you had 
to train on longer context length,  

466
00:45:04,560 --> 00:45:10,080
that would mean that you're able to get less 
samples in for the same amount of compute? 

467
00:45:10,880 --> 00:45:14,480
Maybe it's not worth diving deep on that.
I want to get an answer to the  

468
00:45:14,480 --> 00:45:20,640
bigger picture question.
I don't feel a preference  

469
00:45:20,640 --> 00:45:25,120
for a human editor that's been working for 
me for six months versus an AI that's been  

470
00:45:25,120 --> 00:45:31,200
working with me for six months, what year 
do you predict that that will be the case? 

471
00:45:33,840 --> 00:45:38,160
My guess for that is there's a lot of problems 
where basically we can do this when we have  

472
00:45:38,160 --> 00:45:48,080
the "country of geniuses in a data center".
My picture for that, if you made me guess, is  

473
00:45:48,080 --> 00:45:54,160
one to two years, maybe one to three years. It's 
really hard to tell. I have a strong view—99%,  

474
00:45:54,160 --> 00:45:59,920
95%—that all this will happen in 10 years.
I think that's just a super safe bet. 

475
00:46:00,560 --> 00:46:04,720
I have a hunch—this is more like a 50/50 
thing—that it's going to be more like  

476
00:46:04,720 --> 00:46:08,960
one to two, maybe more like one to three.
So one to three years. Country of geniuses,  

477
00:46:10,160 --> 00:46:14,320
and the slightly less economically 
valuable task of editing videos. 

478
00:46:14,320 --> 00:46:17,040
It seems pretty economically 
valuable, let me tell you. 

479
00:46:17,040 --> 00:46:20,640
It's just there are a lot of use cases like that.
There are a lot of similar ones. 

480
00:46:20,640 --> 00:46:23,680
So you're predicting that 
within one to three years. 

481
00:46:23,680 --> 00:46:28,960
And then, generally, Anthropic has predicted that 
by late '26 or early '27 we will have AI systems  

482
00:46:28,960 --> 00:46:34,160
that "have the ability to navigate interfaces 
available to humans doing digital work today,  

483
00:46:34,160 --> 00:46:38,960
intellectual capabilities matching or exceeding 
that of Nobel Prize winners, and the ability to  

484
00:46:38,960 --> 00:46:42,400
interface with the physical world".
You gave an interview two months ago  

485
00:46:42,400 --> 00:46:48,800
with DealBook where you were emphasizing 
your company's more responsible compute  

486
00:46:48,800 --> 00:46:52,400
scaling as compared to your competitors.
I'm trying to square these two views. 

487
00:46:52,960 --> 00:46:57,600
If you really believe that we're going to 
have a country of geniuses, you want as  

488
00:46:57,600 --> 00:47:00,640
big a data center as you can get.
There's no reason to slow down. 

489
00:47:00,640 --> 00:47:04,960
The TAM of a Nobel Prize winner, that 
can actually do everything a Nobel Prize  

490
00:47:04,960 --> 00:47:10,560
winner can do, is trillions of dollars.
So I'm trying to square this conservatism,  

491
00:47:10,560 --> 00:47:16,320
which seems rational if you have more moderate 
timelines, with your stated views about progress. 

492
00:47:16,880 --> 00:47:22,400
It actually all fits together. We go back to 
this fast, but not infinitely fast, diffusion. 

493
00:47:23,120 --> 00:47:29,200
Let's say that we're making progress at this rate.
The technology is making progress this fast. 

494
00:47:29,920 --> 00:47:39,040
I have very high conviction that we're 
going to get there within a few years. 

495
00:47:39,040 --> 00:47:41,440
I have a hunch that we're going 
to get there within a year or two. 

496
00:47:41,440 --> 00:47:46,800
So there’s a little uncertainty on 
the technical side, but pretty strong  

497
00:47:46,800 --> 00:47:51,600
confidence that it won't be off by much.
What I'm less certain about is, again,  

498
00:47:51,600 --> 00:47:56,640
the economic diffusion side.
I really do believe that we could  

499
00:47:56,640 --> 00:48:03,920
have models that are a country of geniuses 
in the data center in one to two years. 

500
00:48:03,920 --> 00:48:13,360
One question is: How many years after that 
do the trillions in revenue start rolling in? 

501
00:48:14,160 --> 00:48:18,080
I don't think it's guaranteed 
that it's going to be immediate. 

502
00:48:19,440 --> 00:48:27,120
It could be one year, it could be two 
years, I could even stretch it to five  

503
00:48:27,120 --> 00:48:35,040
years although I'm skeptical of that. So we have 
this uncertainty. Even if the technology goes as  

504
00:48:35,040 --> 00:48:41,680
fast as I suspect that it will, we don't know 
exactly how fast it's going to drive revenue. 

505
00:48:41,680 --> 00:48:47,360
We know it's coming, but with the way you buy 
these data centers, if you're off by a couple  

506
00:48:47,360 --> 00:48:50,080
years, that can be ruinous.
It is just like how I  

507
00:48:50,080 --> 00:48:55,120
wrote in "Machines of Loving Grace".
I said I think we might get this powerful AI,  

508
00:48:55,120 --> 00:48:57,920
this "country of genius in the data center".
That description you gave comes  

509
00:48:57,920 --> 00:49:02,800
from "Machines of Loving Grace".
I said we'll get that in 2026, maybe 2027. Again,  

510
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:08,160
that is my hunch. I wouldn't be surprised if 
I'm off by a year or two, but that is my hunch.  

511
00:49:08,160 --> 00:49:12,720
Let's say that happens. That's the starting gun. 
How long does it take to cure all the diseases? 

512
00:49:13,360 --> 00:49:21,120
That's one of the ways that drives a huge amount 
of economic value. You cure every disease. There's  

513
00:49:21,120 --> 00:49:24,000
a question of how much of that goes to the 
pharmaceutical company or the AI company,  

514
00:49:24,000 --> 00:49:29,280
but there's an enormous consumer surplus because 
—assuming we can get access for everyone,  

515
00:49:29,280 --> 00:49:34,240
which I care about greatly—we cure all of 
these diseases. How long does it take? You  

516
00:49:34,240 --> 00:49:40,960
have to do the biological discovery, 
you have to manufacture the new drug,  

517
00:49:40,960 --> 00:49:45,200
you have to go through the regulatory process.
We saw this with vaccines and COVID. 

518
00:49:47,680 --> 00:49:51,120
We got the vaccine out to everyone, 
but it took a year and a half. 

519
00:49:52,000 --> 00:49:58,480
My question is: How long does it take to get 
the cure for everything—which AI is the genius  

520
00:49:58,480 --> 00:50:03,040
that can in theory invent—out to everyone?
How long from when that AI first exists  

521
00:50:03,040 --> 00:50:08,080
in the lab to when diseases have 
actually been cured for everyone? 

522
00:50:09,920 --> 00:50:14,560
We've had a polio vaccine for 50 years.
We're still trying to eradicate it in the  

523
00:50:14,560 --> 00:50:18,160
most remote corners of Africa.
The Gates Foundation is trying  

524
00:50:18,160 --> 00:50:20,080
as hard as they can.
Others are trying as hard  

525
00:50:20,080 --> 00:50:25,680
as they can. But that's difficult. Again, I 
don't expect most of the economic diffusion  

526
00:50:25,680 --> 00:50:32,480
to be as difficult as that. That's the most 
difficult case. But there's a real dilemma here. 

527
00:50:32,480 --> 00:50:39,120
Where I've settled on it is that it will 
be faster than anything we've seen in the  

528
00:50:39,120 --> 00:50:45,920
world, but it still has its limits.
So when we go to buying data centers,  

529
00:50:47,120 --> 00:50:54,080
again, the curve I'm looking at is: we've 
had a 10x a year increase every year. 

530
00:50:54,080 --> 00:51:00,880
At the beginning of this year, we're looking 
at $10 billion in annualized revenue. 

531
00:51:02,320 --> 00:51:10,800
We have to decide how much compute to buy.
It takes a year or two to actually build out  

532
00:51:10,800 --> 00:51:16,960
the data centers, to reserve the data center.
Basically I'm saying, "In 2027,  

533
00:51:16,960 --> 00:51:24,720
how much compute do I get?"
I could assume that the  

534
00:51:24,720 --> 00:51:31,200
revenue will continue growing 10x a year, 
so it'll be $100 billion at the end of  

535
00:51:31,200 --> 00:51:39,520
2026 and $1 trillion at the end of 2027.
Actually it would be $5 trillion dollars  

536
00:51:39,520 --> 00:51:43,600
of compute because it would be $1 
trillion a year for five years. 

537
00:51:43,600 --> 00:51:48,480
I could buy $1 trillion of compute 
that starts at the end of 2027. 

538
00:51:49,200 --> 00:51:56,320
If my revenue is not $1 trillion dollars, if it's 
even $800 billion, there's no force on earth,  

539
00:51:56,320 --> 00:52:02,000
there's no hedge on earth that could stop me 
from going bankrupt if I buy that much compute. 

540
00:52:03,200 --> 00:52:07,280
Even though a part of my brain wonders 
if it's going to keep growing 10x,  

541
00:52:07,280 --> 00:52:17,200
I can't buy $1 trillion a year of compute in 2027.
If I'm just off by a year in that rate of growth,  

542
00:52:17,200 --> 00:52:23,760
or if the growth rate is 5x a year instead 
of 10x a year, then you go bankrupt. 

543
00:52:25,360 --> 00:52:32,240
So you end up in a world where you're 
supporting hundreds of billions, not trillions. 

544
00:52:33,120 --> 00:52:38,560
You accept some risk that there's so much 
demand that you can't support the revenue,  

545
00:52:38,560 --> 00:52:43,680
and you accept some risk that you 
got it wrong and it's still slow. 

546
00:52:43,680 --> 00:52:49,120
When I talked about behaving responsibly, what 
I meant actually was not the absolute amount. 

547
00:52:51,920 --> 00:52:55,760
I think it is true we're spending somewhat 
less than some of the other players. 

548
00:52:55,760 --> 00:53:01,840
It's actually the other things, like have we been 
thoughtful about it or are we YOLOing and saying,  

549
00:53:01,840 --> 00:53:05,200
"We're going to do $100 billion 
here or $100 billion there"? 

550
00:53:05,200 --> 00:53:09,680
I get the impression that some of the 
other companies have not written down  

551
00:53:09,680 --> 00:53:12,640
the spreadsheet, that they don't really 
understand the risks they're taking. 

552
00:53:12,640 --> 00:53:19,200
They're just doing stuff because it sounds 
cool. We've thought carefully about it. We're  

553
00:53:19,200 --> 00:53:26,240
an enterprise business. Therefore, we can rely 
more on revenue. It's less fickle than consumer.  

554
00:53:26,240 --> 00:53:31,520
We have better margins, which is the buffer 
between buying too much and buying too little. 

555
00:53:31,520 --> 00:53:37,200
I think we bought an amount that allows 
us to capture pretty strong upside worlds. 

556
00:53:37,200 --> 00:53:42,240
It won't capture the full 10x a year.
Things would have to go pretty badly for  

557
00:53:42,240 --> 00:53:46,480
us to be in financial trouble.
So we've thought carefully and  

558
00:53:46,480 --> 00:53:48,880
we've made that balance.
That's what I mean when  

559
00:53:48,880 --> 00:53:54,080
I say that we're being responsible.
So it seems like it's possible that we  

560
00:53:54,080 --> 00:53:56,960
actually just have different definitions of 
the "country of a genius in a data center". 

561
00:53:56,960 --> 00:54:02,560
Because when I think of actual human geniuses, an 
actual country of human geniuses in a data center,  

562
00:54:02,560 --> 00:54:08,320
I would happily buy $5 trillion worth 
of compute to run an actual country of  

563
00:54:08,320 --> 00:54:11,360
human geniuses in a data center.
Let's say JPMorgan or Moderna or  

564
00:54:11,360 --> 00:54:14,640
whatever doesn't want to use them.
I've got a country of geniuses.  

565
00:54:14,640 --> 00:54:18,080
They'll start their own company. If they can't 
start their own company and they're bottlenecked  

566
00:54:18,080 --> 00:54:22,240
by clinical trials… It is worth stating that with 
clinical trials, most clinical trials fail because  

567
00:54:22,240 --> 00:54:27,200
the drug doesn't work. There's not efficacy.
I make exactly that point in "Machines of  

568
00:54:27,200 --> 00:54:30,160
Loving Grace", I say the clinical 
trials are going to go much faster  

569
00:54:30,160 --> 00:54:35,760
than we're used to, but not infinitely fast.
Okay, and then suppose it takes a year for  

570
00:54:35,760 --> 00:54:39,040
the clinical trials to work out so that you're 
getting revenue from that and can make more drugs. 

