01:13:37,200 --> 01:13:41,040
The way you get industries in which 
there are a small number of players,  

762
01:13:41,040 --> 01:13:49,440
is very high costs of entry. Cloud is like 
this. I think cloud is a good example of this. 

763
01:13:49,440 --> 01:13:55,120
There are three, maybe four, players within cloud.
I think that's the same for AI, three, maybe four. 

764
01:13:56,640 --> 01:14:02,240
The reason is that it's so expensive.
It requires so much expertise and so  

765
01:14:02,240 --> 01:14:08,240
much capital to run a cloud company.
You have to put up all this capital. 

766
01:14:08,240 --> 01:14:11,840
In addition to putting up all this capital, 
you have to get all of this other stuff  

767
01:14:11,840 --> 01:14:17,440
that requires a lot of skill to make it happen.
So if you go to someone and you're like, "I want  

768
01:14:17,440 --> 01:14:22,640
to disrupt this industry, here's $100 billion."
You're like, "okay, I'm putting in $100 billion  

769
01:14:22,640 --> 01:14:26,312
and also betting that you can do all these 
other things that these people have been doing." 

770
01:14:26,312 --> 01:14:29,360
Only to decrease the profit.
The effect of your entering  

771
01:14:29,360 --> 01:14:33,280
is that profit margins go down.
So, we have equilibria like this  

772
01:14:33,280 --> 01:14:39,840
all the time in the economy where we have a few 
players. Profits are not astronomical. Margins  

773
01:14:39,840 --> 01:14:47,280
are not astronomical, but they're not zero.
That's what we see on cloud. Cloud is very  

774
01:14:47,280 --> 01:14:50,800
undifferentiated. Models are 
more differentiated than cloud. 

775
01:14:51,680 --> 01:14:58,960
Everyone knows Claude is good at different things 
than GPT is good at, than Gemini is good at. 

776
01:14:58,960 --> 01:15:03,360
It's not just that Claude's good at 
coding, GPT is good at math and reasoning.  

777
01:15:05,120 --> 01:15:09,680
It's more subtle than that. Models are good at 
different types of coding. Models have different  

778
01:15:09,680 --> 01:15:15,680
styles. I think these things are actually quite 
different from each other, and so I would expect  

779
01:15:15,680 --> 01:15:26,720
more differentiation than you see in cloud.
Now, there actually is one counter-argument. 

780
01:15:26,720 --> 01:15:30,320
That counter-argument is if the 
process of producing models,  

781
01:15:32,960 --> 01:15:37,600
if AI models can do that themselves, then 
that could spread throughout the economy. 

782
01:15:37,600 --> 01:15:41,280
But that is not an argument for 
commoditizing AI models in general. 

783
01:15:41,280 --> 01:15:45,440
That's kind of an argument for 
commoditizing the whole economy at once. 

784
01:15:45,440 --> 01:15:48,960
I don't know what quite happens in 
that world where basically anyone  

785
01:15:48,960 --> 01:15:53,760
can do anything, anyone can build anything, 
and there's no moat around anything at all. 

786
01:15:53,760 --> 01:15:58,560
I don't know, maybe we want that world.
Maybe that's the end state here. 

787
01:15:58,560 --> 01:16:09,280
Maybe when AI models can do everything, if we've 
solved all the safety and security problems,  

788
01:16:09,280 --> 01:16:17,920
that's one of the mechanisms for the 
economy just flattening itself again. 

789
01:16:17,920 --> 01:16:22,480
But that's kind of far post-"country 
of geniuses in the data center." 

790
01:16:23,440 --> 01:16:32,480
Maybe a finer way to put that potential point 
is: 1) it seems like AI research is especially  

791
01:16:32,480 --> 01:16:37,760
loaded on raw intellectual power, which will 
be especially abundant in the world of AGI. 

792
01:16:37,760 --> 01:16:41,920
And 2) if you just look at the world today, 
there are very few technologies that seem to be  

793
01:16:41,920 --> 01:16:50,080
diffusing as fast as AI algorithmic progress.
So that does hint that this industry is  

794
01:16:50,080 --> 01:16:54,240
sort of structurally diffusive.
I think coding is going fast, but  

795
01:16:54,240 --> 01:16:58,640
I think AI research is a superset of coding and 
there are aspects of it that are not going fast. 

796
01:17:00,320 --> 01:17:07,360
But I do think, again, once we get coding, once we 
get AI models going fast, then that will speed up  

797
01:17:07,360 --> 01:17:13,600
the ability of AI models to do everything else.
So while coding is going fast now, I think once  

798
01:17:13,600 --> 01:17:17,520
the AI models are building the next AI 
models and building everything else,  

799
01:17:17,520 --> 01:17:24,720
the whole economy will kind of go at the same 
pace. I am worried geographically, though.  

800
01:17:24,720 --> 01:17:34,400
I'm a little worried that just proximity to AI, 
having heard about AI, may be one differentiator. 

801
01:17:34,400 --> 01:17:42,160
So when I said the 10-20% growth rate, a worry 
I have is that the growth rate could be like 50%  

802
01:17:42,160 --> 01:17:50,160
in Silicon Valley and parts of the world that are 
socially connected to Silicon Valley, and not that  

803
01:17:50,160 --> 01:17:54,160
much faster than its current pace elsewhere.
I think that'd be a pretty messed up world. 

804
01:17:54,160 --> 01:17:57,280
So one of the things I think about 
a lot is how to prevent that. 

805
01:17:57,280 --> 01:18:01,920
Do you think that once we have this 
country of geniuses in a data center, that  

806
01:18:01,920 --> 01:18:06,240
robotics is sort of quickly solved afterwards?
Because it seems like a big problem with robotics  

807
01:18:06,240 --> 01:18:12,800
is that a human can learn how to teleoperate 
current hardware, but current AI models can't,  

808
01:18:12,800 --> 01:18:16,880
at least not in a way that's super productive.
And so if we have this ability to learn like  

809
01:18:16,880 --> 01:18:19,120
a human, shouldn't it solve 
robotics immediately as well? 

810
01:18:19,120 --> 01:18:21,520
I don't think it's dependent 
on learning like a human. 

811
01:18:21,520 --> 01:18:25,440
It could happen in different ways.
Again, we could have trained the model on  

812
01:18:25,440 --> 01:18:30,240
many different video games, which are like robotic 
controls, or many different simulated robotics  

813
01:18:30,240 --> 01:18:34,800
environments, or just train them to control 
computer screens, and they learn to generalize. 

814
01:18:34,800 --> 01:18:41,680
So it will happen... it's not necessarily 
dependent on human-like learning. 

815
01:18:41,680 --> 01:18:44,960
Human-like learning is one way it could happen.
If the model's like, "Oh, I pick up a robot,  

816
01:18:44,960 --> 01:18:50,720
I don't know how to use it, I learn," that could 
happen because we discovered continual learning. 

817
01:18:50,720 --> 01:18:54,000
That could also happen because we trained 
the model on a bunch of environments and  

818
01:18:54,000 --> 01:18:58,400
then generalized, or it could happen because 
the model learns that in the context length. 

819
01:18:58,960 --> 01:19:03,200
It doesn't actually matter which way.
If we go back to the discussion we had  

820
01:19:03,200 --> 01:19:08,800
an hour ago, that type of thing can 
happen in several different ways. 

821
01:19:10,240 --> 01:19:16,800
But I do think when for whatever reason the 
models have those skills, then robotics will be  

822
01:19:16,800 --> 01:19:21,200
revolutionized—both the design of robots, because 
the models will be much better than humans at  

823
01:19:21,200 --> 01:19:28,800
that, and also the ability to control robots.
So we'll get better at building the physical  

824
01:19:28,800 --> 01:19:32,320
hardware, building the physical robots, and 
we'll also get better at controlling it. 

825
01:19:32,320 --> 01:19:36,800
Now, does that mean the robotics 
industry will also be generating  

826
01:19:36,800 --> 01:19:40,480
trillions of dollars of revenue?
My answer there is yes, but there will be  

827
01:19:40,480 --> 01:19:46,560
the same extremely fast, but not infinitely fast 
diffusion. So will robotics be revolutionized?  

828
01:19:46,560 --> 01:19:51,840
Yeah, maybe tack on another year or two.
That's the way I think about these things. 

829
01:19:52,400 --> 01:19:58,880
Makes sense. There's a general skepticism about 
extremely fast progress. Here's my view. It sounds  

830
01:19:58,880 --> 01:20:02,800
like you are going to solve continual learning 
one way or another within a matter of years. 

831
01:20:02,800 --> 01:20:06,800
But just as people weren't talking about 
continual learning a couple of years ago,  

832
01:20:06,800 --> 01:20:09,920
and then we realized, "Oh, why aren't these 
models as useful as they could be right now,  

833
01:20:09,920 --> 01:20:14,080
even though they are clearly passing the Turing 
test and are experts in so many different domains?  

834
01:20:14,080 --> 01:20:19,840
Maybe it's this thing." Then we solve this thing 
and we realize, actually, there's another thing  

835
01:20:19,840 --> 01:20:24,080
that human intelligence can do that's a basis 
of human labor that these models can't do. 

836
01:20:24,080 --> 01:20:27,600
So why not think there will be 
more things like this, where  

837
01:20:28,560 --> 01:20:33,680
we've found more pieces of human intelligence?
Well, to be clear, I think continual learning, as  

838
01:20:33,680 --> 01:20:40,240
I've said before, might not be a barrier at all.
I think we may just get there by pre-training  

839
01:20:40,240 --> 01:20:45,280
generalization and RL generalization.
I think there just  

840
01:20:48,240 --> 01:20:51,920
might not be such a thing at all.
In fact, I would point to the history  

841
01:20:51,920 --> 01:20:56,720
in ML of people coming up with things 
that are barriers that end up kind of  

842
01:20:56,720 --> 01:21:06,480
dissolving within the big blob of compute.
People talked about, "How do your models  

843
01:21:06,480 --> 01:21:11,920
keep track of nouns and verbs?" 
"They can understand syntactically,  

844
01:21:11,920 --> 01:21:16,720
but they can't understand semantically?
It's only statistical correlations."  

845
01:21:16,720 --> 01:21:19,520
"You can understand a paragraph, 
you can’t understand a word. 

846
01:21:19,520 --> 01:21:23,120
There's reasoning, you can't do reasoning."
But then suddenly it turns out you can  

847
01:21:23,120 --> 01:21:27,840
do code and math very well.
So I think there's actually a  

848
01:21:27,840 --> 01:21:35,440
stronger history of some of these things seeming 
like a big deal and then kind of dissolving. Some  

849
01:21:35,440 --> 01:21:42,560
of them are real. The need for data is real, 
maybe continual learning is a real thing. 

850
01:21:42,560 --> 01:21:45,920
But again, I would ground 
us in something like code. 

851
01:21:46,480 --> 01:21:50,160
I think we may get to the point in 
a year or two where the models can  

852
01:21:50,160 --> 01:21:56,240
just do SWE end-to-end. That's a whole task. 
That's a whole sphere of human activity that  

853
01:21:56,240 --> 01:22:00,800
we're just saying models can do now.
When you say end-to-end, do you mean  

854
01:22:02,000 --> 01:22:06,640
setting technical direction, understanding 
the context of the problem, et cetera? 

855
01:22:06,640 --> 01:22:12,560
Yes. I mean all of that.
Interesting. I feel like that is AGI-complete,  

856
01:22:13,520 --> 01:22:17,280
which maybe is internally consistent.
But it's not like saying 90%  

857
01:22:17,280 --> 01:22:22,720
of code or 100% of code.
No, I gave this spectrum:  

858
01:22:22,720 --> 01:22:28,640
90% of code, 100% of code, 90% of 
end-to-end SWE, 100% of end-to-end SWE. 

859
01:22:28,640 --> 01:22:31,920
New tasks are created for SWEs.
Eventually those get done as well. 

860
01:22:31,920 --> 01:22:35,760
It's a long spectrum there, but we're 
traversing the spectrum very quickly. 

861
01:22:35,760 --> 01:22:39,280
I do think it's funny that I've seen 
a couple of podcasts you've done where  

862
01:22:40,080 --> 01:22:43,680
the hosts will be like, "But Dwarkesh wrote 
the essay about the continuous learning thing." 

863
01:22:43,680 --> 01:22:47,840
It always makes me crack up because 
you've been an AI researcher for 10 years. 

864
01:22:48,880 --> 01:22:53,600
I'm sure there's some feeling of, 
"Okay, so a podcaster wrote an essay,  

865
01:22:53,600 --> 01:22:59,120
and every interview I get asked about it."
The truth of the matter is that we're all  

866
01:22:59,120 --> 01:23:04,800
trying to figure this out together.
There are some ways in which I'm  

867
01:23:04,800 --> 01:23:08,960
able to see things that others aren't.
These days that probably has more to do  

868
01:23:08,960 --> 01:23:13,760
with seeing a bunch of stuff within Anthropic and 
having to make a bunch of decisions than I have  

869
01:23:13,760 --> 01:23:20,160
any great research insight that others don't.
I'm running a 2,500 person company. 

870
01:23:20,160 --> 01:23:27,520
It's actually pretty hard for me to have concrete 
research insight, much harder than it would have  

871
01:23:27,520 --> 01:23:36,000
been 10 years ago or even two or three years ago.
As we go towards a world of a full drop-in  

872
01:23:36,000 --> 01:23:42,160
remote worker replacement, does an API 
pricing model still make the most sense? 

873
01:23:42,160 --> 01:23:45,920
If not, what is the correct 
way to price AGI, or serve AGI? 

874
01:23:45,920 --> 01:23:49,920
I think there's going to be a bunch of 
different business models here, all at once,  

875
01:23:49,920 --> 01:23:57,680
that are going to be experimented with.
I actually do think that the API  

876
01:23:59,680 --> 01:24:06,960
model is more durable than many people think.
One way I think about it is if the technology  

877
01:24:06,960 --> 01:24:12,080
is advancing quickly, if it's advancing 
exponentially, what that means is there's  

878
01:24:12,080 --> 01:24:20,080
always a surface area of new use cases that 
have been developed in the last three months. 

879
01:24:20,080 --> 01:24:27,840
Any kind of product surface you put in place is 
always at risk of sort of becoming irrelevant. 

880
01:24:27,840 --> 01:24:32,880
Any given product surface probably makes sense 
for a range of capabilities of the model. 

881
01:24:32,880 --> 01:24:39,520
The chatbot is already running into limitations 
where making it smarter doesn't really help the  

882
01:24:39,520 --> 01:24:41,920
average consumer that much.
But I don't think that's  

883
01:24:41,920 --> 01:24:45,440
a limitation of AI models.
I don't think that's evidence  

884
01:24:45,440 --> 01:24:51,440
that the models are good enough and them 
getting better doesn't matter to the economy. 

885
01:24:51,440 --> 01:24:58,720
It doesn't matter to that particular product.
So I think the value of the API is that the API  

886
01:24:58,720 --> 01:25:05,360
always offers an opportunity, very close to the 
bare metal, to build on what the latest thing is. 

887
01:25:06,720 --> 01:25:14,400
There's always going to be this front 
of new startups and new ideas that  

888
01:25:14,400 --> 01:25:18,400
weren't possible a few months ago and are 
possible because the model is advancing. 

889
01:25:19,520 --> 01:25:28,640
I actually predict that it's going to exist 
alongside other models, but we're always going  

890
01:25:28,640 --> 01:25:34,720
to have the API business model because there's 
always going to be a need for a thousand different  

891
01:25:34,720 --> 01:25:40,160
people to try experimenting with the model in a 
different way. 100 of them become startups and  

892
01:25:40,160 --> 01:25:45,040
ten of them become big successful startups.
Two or three really end up being the way  

893
01:25:45,040 --> 01:25:50,960
that people use the model of a given generation.
So I basically think it's always going to exist. 

894
01:25:50,960 --> 01:25:55,760
At the same time, I'm sure there's 
going to be other models as well. 

895
01:25:55,760 --> 01:26:00,400
Not every token that's output by 
the model is worth the same amount. 

896
01:26:00,400 --> 01:26:10,800
Think about what is the value of the tokens 
that the model outputs when someone calls  

897
01:26:10,800 --> 01:26:14,480
them up and says, "My Mac isn't working," or 
something, the model's like, "restart it." 

898
01:26:16,160 --> 01:26:22,240
Someone hasn't heard that before, but 
the model said that 10 million times. 

899
01:26:23,280 --> 01:26:26,080
Maybe that's worth like a dollar 
or a few cents or something. 

900
01:26:26,880 --> 01:26:34,800
Whereas if the model goes to one of the 
pharmaceutical companies and it says, "Oh,  

901
01:26:34,800 --> 01:26:39,360
you know, this molecule you're developing, you 
should take the aromatic ring from that end of the  

902
01:26:39,360 --> 01:26:45,120
molecule and put it on that end of the molecule.
If you do that, wonderful things will happen." 

903
01:26:46,800 --> 01:26:50,240
Those tokens could be worth 
tens of millions of dollars. 

904
01:26:52,400 --> 01:26:56,880
So I think we're definitely going to 
see business models that recognize that. 

905
01:26:56,880 --> 01:27:06,720
At some point we're going to see "pay for results" 
in some form, or we may see forms of compensation  

906
01:27:06,720 --> 01:27:16,880
that are like labor, that kind of work by the 
hour. I don't know. I think because it's a new  

907
01:27:16,880 --> 01:27:19,920
industry, a lot of things are going to be tried.
I don't know what will turn out to  

908
01:27:19,920 --> 01:27:24,480
be the right thing.
I take your point that  

909
01:27:24,480 --> 01:27:28,960
people will have to try things to figure out what 
is the best way to use this blob of intelligence. 

910
01:27:28,960 --> 01:27:34,800
But what I find striking is Claude Code.
I don't think in the history of startups  

911
01:27:34,800 --> 01:27:40,880
there has been a single application that has 
been as hotly competed in as coding agents. 

912
01:27:42,960 --> 01:27:49,920
Claude Code is a category leader here. That 
seems surprising to me. It doesn't seem  

913
01:27:49,920 --> 01:27:54,720
intrinsically that Anthropic had to build this.
I wonder if you have an accounting of why it had  

914
01:27:54,720 --> 01:27:58,160
to be Anthropic or how Anthropic ended 
up building an application in addition  

915
01:27:58,160 --> 01:28:02,000
to the model underlying it that was successful.
So it actually happened in a pretty simple way,  

916
01:28:02,000 --> 01:28:08,640
which is that we had our own coding 
models, which were good at coding. 

917
01:28:09,440 --> 01:28:14,400
Around the beginning of 2025, I said, "I 
think the time has come where you can have  

918
01:28:14,400 --> 01:28:21,600
nontrivial acceleration of your own research 
if you're an AI company by using these models." 

919
01:28:21,600 --> 01:28:25,520
Of course, you need an interface, 
you need a harness to use them.  

920
01:28:25,520 --> 01:28:31,520
So I encouraged people internally. I didn't 
say this is one thing that you have to use. 

921
01:28:31,520 --> 01:28:37,760
I just said people should experiment with this.
I think it might have been originally  

922
01:28:37,760 --> 01:28:42,080
called Claude CLI, and then the name 
eventually got changed to Claude Code. 

923
01:28:42,080 --> 01:28:48,480
Internally, it was the thing that everyone was 
using and it was seeing fast internal adoption. 

924
01:28:48,480 --> 01:28:52,160
I looked at it and I said, "Probably we 
should launch this externally, right?" 

925
01:28:53,200 --> 01:28:59,040
It's seen such fast adoption within Anthropic.
Coding is a lot of what we do. 

926
01:28:59,920 --> 01:29:04,640
We have an audience of many, many hundreds 
of people that's in some ways at least  

927
01:29:04,640 --> 01:29:08,400
representative of the external audience.
So it looks like we already have product  

928
01:29:08,400 --> 01:29:15,360
market fit. Let's launch this thing. And then 
we launched it. I think just the fact that we  

929
01:29:15,360 --> 01:29:21,280
ourselves are kind of developing the model and we 
ourselves know what we most need to use the model,  

930
01:29:21,280 --> 01:29:26,400
I think it's kind of creating this feedback loop.
I see. In the sense that you, let's say a  

931
01:29:26,400 --> 01:29:31,680
developer at Anthropic is like, "Ah, it would 
be better if it was better at this X thing." 

932
01:29:31,680 --> 01:29:35,120
Then you bake that into the 
next model that you build. 

933
01:29:35,120 --> 01:29:39,200
That's one version of it, but then there's 
just the ordinary product iteration. 

934
01:29:41,520 --> 01:29:47,280
We have a bunch of coders within 
Anthropic, they use Claude Code  

935
01:29:47,280 --> 01:29:50,320
every day and so we get fast feedback.
That was more important in the early days. 

936
01:29:50,320 --> 01:29:53,600
Now, of course, there are millions 
of people using it, and so we get  

937
01:29:53,600 --> 01:29:58,160
a bunch of external feedback as well.
But it's just great to be able to get  

938
01:29:58,160 --> 01:30:03,920
kind of fast internal feedback.
I think this is the reason why we  

939
01:30:03,920 --> 01:30:07,840
launched a coding model and didn't 
launch a pharmaceutical company. 

940
01:30:10,560 --> 01:30:14,640
My background's in biology, but we 
don't have any of the resources that  

941
01:30:14,640 --> 01:31:23,440
are needed to launch a pharmaceutical company.
Let me now ask you about making AI go well. 

942
01:31:24,320 --> 01:31:30,160
It seems like whatever vision we have about how 
AI goes well has to be compatible with two things:  

943
01:31:30,160 --> 01:31:37,440
1) the ability to build and run AIs is 
diffusing extremely rapidly and 2) the  

944
01:31:37,440 --> 01:31:43,200
population of AIs, the amount we have and their 
intelligence, will also increase very rapidly. 

945
01:31:44,160 --> 01:31:49,360
That means that lots of people will be able 
to build huge populations of misaligned AIs,  

946
01:31:49,360 --> 01:31:53,520
or AIs which are just companies 
which are trying to increase their  

947
01:31:53,520 --> 01:31:57,920
footprint or have weird psyches like 
Sydney Bing, but now they're superhuman. 

948
01:31:57,920 --> 01:32:02,880
What is a vision for a world in which we 
have an equilibrium that is compatible  

949
01:32:02,880 --> 01:32:05,920
with lots of different AIs, some of 
which are misaligned, running around? 

950
01:32:06,720 --> 01:32:13,680
I think in "The Adolescence of Technology", 
I was skeptical of the balance of power. 

951
01:32:13,680 --> 01:32:22,160
But the thing I was specifically skeptical of 
is you have three or four of these companies  

952
01:32:23,520 --> 01:32:36,720
all building models that are derived from the 
same thing, that they would check each other. 

953
01:32:36,720 --> 01:32:40,880
Or even that any number of 
them would check each other. 

954
01:32:40,880 --> 01:32:47,680
We might live in an offense-dominant world where 
one person or one AI model is smart enough to do  

955
01:32:47,680 --> 01:32:54,400
something that causes damage for everything else.
In the short run, we have a limited number  

956
01:32:54,400 --> 01:32:56,160
of players now.
So we can start  

957
01:32:56,160 --> 01:33:03,680
within the limited number of players.
We need to put in place the safeguards. 

958
01:33:03,680 --> 01:33:05,440
We need to make sure everyone 
does the right alignment work. 

959
01:33:05,440 --> 01:33:11,840
We need to make sure everyone has bioclassifiers.
Those are the immediate things we need to do. 

960
01:33:11,840 --> 01:33:16,880
I agree that that doesn't solve the problem in 
the long run, particularly if the ability of  

961
01:33:16,880 --> 01:33:26,080
AI models to make other AI models proliferates, 
then the whole thing can become harder to solve. 

962
01:33:26,720 --> 01:33:30,800
I think in the long run we need 
some architecture of governance. 

963
01:33:30,800 --> 01:33:35,040
We need some architecture of governance 
that preserves human freedom,  

964
01:33:35,040 --> 01:33:47,920
but also allows us to govern a very large 
number of human systems, AI systems, hybrid  

965
01:33:52,560 --> 01:34:00,000
human-AI companies or economic units.
So we're gonna need to think about:  

966
01:34:01,520 --> 01:34:09,840
how do we protect the world against bioterrorism?
How do we protect the world against mirror life? 

967
01:34:11,200 --> 01:34:15,680
Probably we're gonna need some 
kind of AI monitoring system  

968
01:34:15,680 --> 01:34:20,640
that monitors for all of these things.
But then we need to build this in a way  

969
01:34:20,640 --> 01:34:24,800
that preserves civil liberties 
and our constitutional rights. 

970
01:34:24,800 --> 01:34:34,640
So I think just as anything else, it's a 
new security landscape with a new set of  

971
01:34:34,640 --> 01:34:40,800
tools and a new set of vulnerabilities.
My worry is, if we had 100 years for this  

972
01:34:40,800 --> 01:34:49,040
to happen all very slowly, we'd get used to it.
We've gotten used to the presence of explosives  

973
01:34:49,040 --> 01:34:58,320
in society or the presence of various new 
weapons or the presence of video cameras. 

974
01:34:58,320 --> 01:35:03,120
We would get used to it over 100 years and 
we’d develop governance mechanisms. We'd  

975
01:35:03,120 --> 01:35:07,600
make our mistakes. My worry is just 
that this is happening all so fast. 

976
01:35:07,600 --> 01:35:12,560
So maybe we need to do our thinking faster about 
how to make these governance mechanisms work. 

977
01:35:13,280 --> 01:35:19,440
It seems like in an offense-dominant world, over 
the course of the next century—the idea is that AI  

978
01:35:19,440 --> 01:35:22,960
is making the progress that would happen over the 
next century happen in some period of five to ten  

979
01:35:22,960 --> 01:35:29,280
years—we would still need the same mechanisms, or 
balance of power would be similarly intractable,  

980
01:35:29,280 --> 01:35:36,320
even if humans were the only game in town.
I guess we have the advice of AI. 

981
01:35:36,320 --> 01:35:41,440
But it fundamentally doesn't seem like 
a totally different ball game here. 

982
01:35:41,440 --> 01:35:44,240
If checks and balances were going to 
work, they would work with humans as well. 

983
01:35:44,240 --> 01:35:46,480
If they aren't going to work, they 
wouldn't work with AIs as well. 

984
01:35:47,520 --> 01:35:50,480
So maybe this just dooms human 
checks and balances as well. 

985
01:35:51,440 --> 01:35:55,520
Again, I think there's some 
way to make this happen. 

986
01:35:58,240 --> 01:36:01,360
The governments of the world may have 
to work together to make it happen. 

987
01:36:02,400 --> 01:36:10,240
We may have to talk to AIs about building 
societal structures in such a way that these  

988
01:36:10,240 --> 01:36:15,920
defenses are possible. I don't know. I don’t 
want to say this is so far ahead in time,  

989
01:36:15,920 --> 01:36:21,440
but it’s so far ahead in technological ability 
that may happen over a short period of time,  

990
01:36:21,440 --> 01:36:25,600
that it's hard for us to anticipate it in advance.
Speaking of governments getting involved,  

991
01:36:25,600 --> 01:36:31,680
on December 26, the Tennessee legislature 
introduced a bill which said, "It would  

992
01:36:31,680 --> 01:36:34,880
be an offense for a person to knowingly 
train artificial intelligence to provide  

993
01:36:34,880 --> 01:36:39,440
emotional support, including through 
open-ended conversations with a user." 

994
01:36:39,440 --> 01:36:48,880
Of course, one of the things that Claude attempts 
to do is be a thoughtful, knowledgeable friend. 

995
01:36:48,880 --> 01:36:52,160
In general, it seems like we're going 
to have this patchwork of state laws. 

996
01:36:52,160 --> 01:36:56,160
A lot of the benefits that normal people could 
experience as a result of AI are going to be  

997
01:36:56,160 --> 01:36:59,360
curtailed, especially when we get into the 
kinds of things you discuss in "Machines  

998
01:36:59,360 --> 01:37:02,880
of Loving Grace": biological freedom, 
mental health improvements, et cetera. 

999
01:37:02,880 --> 01:37:10,000
It seems easy to imagine worlds in which these 
get Whac-A-Moled away by different laws, whereas  

1000
01:37:10,000 --> 01:37:15,920
bills like this don't seem to address the actual 
existential threats that you're concerned about. 

1001
01:37:15,920 --> 01:37:20,320
I'm curious to understand, in the context 
of things like this, Anthropic's position  

1002
01:37:20,320 --> 01:37:27,120
against the federal moratorium on state AI laws.
There are many different things going on at once. 

1003
01:37:28,960 --> 01:37:34,640
I think that particular law is dumb.
It was clearly made by legislators  

1004
01:37:34,640 --> 01:37:38,400
who just probably had little idea 
what AI models could do and not do. 

1005
01:37:38,400 --> 01:37:41,040
They're like, "AI models serving 
us, that just sounds scary. 

1006
01:37:41,840 --> 01:37:46,720
I don't want that to happen."
So we're not in favor of that. 

1007
01:37:47,280 --> 01:37:52,480
But that wasn't the thing that was being voted on.
The thing that was being voted on is:  

1008
01:37:52,480 --> 01:38:00,000
we're going to ban all state regulation of AI 
for 10 years with no apparent plan to do any  

1009
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:04,960
federal regulation of AI, which would take 
Congress to pass, which is a very high bar. 

1010
01:38:05,680 --> 01:38:11,040
So the idea that we'd ban states from doing 
anything for 10 years… People said they had  

1011
01:38:11,040 --> 01:38:15,440
a plan for the federal government, but there 
was no actual proposal on the table. There was  

1012
01:38:15,440 --> 01:38:22,400
no actual attempt. Given the serious dangers 
that I lay out in "Adolescence of Technology"  

1013
01:38:22,400 --> 01:38:29,200
around things like biological weapons 
and bioterrorism autonomy risk, and the  

1014
01:38:29,200 --> 01:38:36,640
timelines we've been talking about—10 years is 
an eternity—I think that's a crazy thing to do. 

1015
01:38:36,640 --> 01:38:42,160
So if that's the choice, if that's what 
you force us to choose, then we're going  

1016
01:38:42,160 --> 01:38:47,760
to choose not to have that moratorium.
I think the benefits of that position  

1017
01:38:47,760 --> 01:38:51,440
exceed the costs, but it's not a 
perfect position if that's the choice. 

1018
01:38:51,440 --> 01:38:56,320
Now, I think the thing that we should do, the 
thing that I would support, is the federal  

1019
01:38:56,320 --> 01:39:02,000
government should step in, not saying "states you 
can't regulate", but "Here's what we're going to  

1020
01:39:02,000 --> 01:39:08,960
do, and states you can't differ from this."
I think preemption is fine in the sense of  

1021
01:39:08,960 --> 01:39:12,960
saying that the federal government says, "Here 
is our standard. This applies to everyone.  

1022
01:39:12,960 --> 01:39:16,160
States can't do something different." 
That would be something I would support  

1023
01:39:16,160 --> 01:39:22,000
if it would be done in the right way.
But this idea of states, "You can't do  

1024
01:39:22,000 --> 01:39:29,680
anything and we're not doing anything either," 
that struck us as very much not making sense. 

1025
01:39:29,680 --> 01:39:33,520
I think it will not age well, it is 
already starting to not age well with  

1026
01:39:33,520 --> 01:39:38,240
all the backlash that you've seen.
Now, in terms of what we would want,  

1027
01:39:39,200 --> 01:39:46,000
the things we've talked about are starting with 
transparency standards in order to monitor some  

1028
01:39:46,000 --> 01:39:53,120
of these autonomy risks and bioterrorism risks.
As the risks become more serious, as we get more  

1029
01:39:53,120 --> 01:39:58,720
evidence for them, then I think we could be more 
aggressive in some targeted ways and say, "Hey,  

1030
01:39:58,720 --> 01:40:04,400
AI bioterrorism is really a threat.
Let's pass a law that forces  

1031
01:40:04,400 --> 01:40:07,920
people to have classifiers."
I could even imagine… It depends. 

1032
01:40:07,920 --> 01:40:12,240
It depends how serious the threat it ends up 
being. We don't know for sure. We need to pursue  

1033
01:40:12,240 --> 01:40:16,480
this in an intellectually honest way where we say 
that ahead of time, the risk has not emerged yet. 

1034
01:40:16,480 --> 01:40:20,400
But I could certainly imagine, with 
the pace that things are going at,  

1035
01:40:21,440 --> 01:40:27,040
a world where later this year we say, "Hey, 
this AI bioterrorism stuff is really serious. 

1036
01:40:27,040 --> 01:40:31,600
We should do something about it.
We should put it in a federal standard. 

1037
01:40:31,600 --> 01:40:36,000
If the federal government won't act, we should put 
it in a state standard." I could totally see that. 

1038
01:40:36,000 --> 01:40:42,960
I'm concerned about a world where if you just 
consider the pace of progress you're expecting,  

1039
01:40:42,960 --> 01:40:48,480
the life cycle of legislation...
The benefits are, as you say because  

1040
01:40:48,480 --> 01:40:55,920
of diffusion lag, slow enough that I 
really do think this patchwork of state  

1041
01:40:55,920 --> 01:40:59,840
laws, on the current trajectory, would prohibit.
I mean if having an emotional chatbot friend is  

1042
01:40:59,840 --> 01:41:03,680
something that freaks people out, then just 
imagine the kinds of actual benefits from AI  

1043
01:41:03,680 --> 01:41:08,320
we want normal people to be able to experience.
From improvements in health and healthspan and  

1044
01:41:08,320 --> 01:41:13,360
improvements in mental health and so forth.
Whereas at the same time, it seems like you  

1045
01:41:13,360 --> 01:41:19,600
think the dangers are already on the horizon and 
I just don't see that much… It seems like it would  

1046
01:41:19,600 --> 01:41:24,080
be especially injurious to the benefits 
of AI as compared to the dangers of AI. 

1047
01:41:24,080 --> 01:41:27,680
So that's maybe where the cost 
benefit makes less sense to me. 

1048
01:41:27,680 --> 01:41:31,280
So there's a few things here.
People talk about there being  

1049
01:41:31,280 --> 01:41:34,400
thousands of these state laws.
First of all, the vast,  

1050
01:41:34,400 --> 01:41:41,040
vast majority of them do not pass.
The world works a certain way in theory,  

1051
01:41:41,040 --> 01:41:44,640
but just because a law has been passed 
doesn't mean it's really enforced. 

1052
01:41:44,640 --> 01:41:48,880
The people implementing it may be 
like, "Oh my God, this is stupid. 

1053
01:41:48,880 --> 01:41:54,800
It would mean shutting off everything 
that's ever been built in Tennessee." 

1054
01:41:55,440 --> 01:42:02,640
Very often, laws are interpreted in a way 
that makes them not as dangerous or harmful. 

1055
01:42:02,640 --> 01:42:06,640
On the same side, of course, you have to worry 
if you're passing a law to stop a bad thing;  

1056
01:42:06,640 --> 01:42:16,400
you have this problem as well.
My basic view is that if we could  

1057
01:42:16,400 --> 01:42:21,520
decide what laws were passed and how things 
were done—and we’re only one small input  

1058
01:42:21,520 --> 01:42:28,160
into that—I would deregulate a lot of the 
stuff around the health benefits of AI. 

1059
01:42:29,840 --> 01:42:37,280
I don't worry as much about the chatbot laws.
I actually worry more about the drug approval  

1060
01:42:37,280 --> 01:42:45,040
process, where I think AI models are going to 
greatly accelerate the rate at which we discover  

1061
01:42:45,040 --> 01:42:50,880
drugs, and the pipeline will get jammed up.
The pipeline will not be prepared to process  

1062
01:42:50,880 --> 01:42:58,720
all the stuff that's going through it.
I think reform of the regulatory process  

1063
01:42:58,720 --> 01:43:02,720
should bias more towards the fact that we have 
a lot of things coming where the safety and  

1064
01:43:02,720 --> 01:43:11,200
efficacy is actually going to be really crisp and 
clear, a beautiful thing, and really effective. 

1065
01:43:12,400 --> 01:43:21,360
Maybe we don't need all this superstructure around 
it that was designed around an era of drugs that  

1066
01:43:21,360 --> 01:43:26,560
barely work and often have serious side effects.
At the same time, I think we should be  

1067
01:43:26,560 --> 01:43:34,880
ramping up quite significantly the 
safety and security legislation. 

1068
01:43:35,520 --> 01:43:43,120
Like I've said, starting with transparency is 
my view of trying not to hamper the industry,  

1069
01:43:43,120 --> 01:43:46,800
trying to find the right balance. I'm 
worried about it. Some people criticize  

1070
01:43:46,800 --> 01:43:50,960
my essay for saying, "That's too slow.
The dangers of AI will come too soon  

1071
01:43:50,960 --> 01:43:52,800
if we do that."
Well, basically,  

1072
01:43:52,800 --> 01:43:58,560
I think the last six months and maybe the next 
few months are going to be about transparency. 

1073
01:43:58,560 --> 01:44:02,880
Then, if these risks emerge when 
we're more certain of them—which  

1074
01:44:02,880 --> 01:44:07,920
I think we might be as soon as later this 
year—then I think we need to act very fast  

1075
01:44:07,920 --> 01:44:13,120
in the areas where we've actually seen the risk.
I think the only way to do this is to be nimble. 

1076
01:44:13,120 --> 01:44:21,280
Now, the legislative process is normally 
not nimble, but we need to emphasize the  

1077
01:44:21,280 --> 01:44:24,240
urgency of this to everyone involved.
That's why I'm sending this message of urgency. 

1078
01:44:24,240 --> 01:44:30,880
That's why I wrote Adolescence of Technology.
I wanted policymakers, economists, national  

1079
01:44:30,880 --> 01:44:36,880
security professionals, and decision-makers to 
read it so that they have some hope of acting  

1080
01:44:36,880 --> 01:44:42,960
faster than they would have otherwise.
Is there anything you can do or advocate  

1081
01:44:42,960 --> 01:44:51,040
that would make it more certain that the 
benefits of AI are better instantiated? 

1082
01:44:51,040 --> 01:44:54,240
I feel like you have worked 
with legislatures to say, "Okay,  

1083
01:44:54,240 --> 01:44:57,120
we're going to prevent bioterrorism here.
We're going to increase transparency, we're  

1084
01:44:57,120 --> 01:45:01,440
going to increase whistleblower protection."
But I think by default, the actual benefits  

1085
01:45:01,440 --> 01:45:08,480
we're looking forward to seem very fragile 
to different kinds of moral panics or  

1086
01:45:08,480 --> 01:45:12,960
political economy problems.
I don't actually agree that  

1087
01:45:12,960 --> 01:45:17,040
much regarding the developed world.
I feel like in the developed world,  

1088
01:45:17,040 --> 01:45:23,520
markets function pretty well.
When there's a lot of money to  

1089
01:45:23,520 --> 01:45:27,680
be made on something and it's clearly the best 
available alternative, it's actually hard for  

1090
01:45:27,680 --> 01:45:32,080
the regulatory system to stop it.
We're seeing that in AI itself. 

1091
01:45:33,600 --> 01:45:37,440
A thing I've been trying to fight for 
is export controls on chips to China. 

1092
01:45:38,640 --> 01:45:41,840
That's in the national 
security interest of the US. 

1093
01:45:42,720 --> 01:45:50,400
That's squarely within the policy beliefs of 
almost everyone in Congress of both parties.  

1094
01:45:52,320 --> 01:45:57,840
The case is very clear. The counterarguments 
against it, I'll politely call them fishy. 

1095
01:45:59,280 --> 01:46:05,680
Yet it doesn't happen and we sell the chips 
because there's so much money riding on it. 

1096
01:46:08,080 --> 01:46:12,160
That money wants to be made.
In that case, in my opinion, that's a bad thing. 

1097
01:46:13,120 --> 01:46:23,040
But it also applies when it's a good thing.
So if we're talking about drugs and benefits of  

1098
01:46:23,040 --> 01:46:30,480
the technology, I am not as worried about those 
benefits being hampered in the developed world. 

1099
01:46:30,480 --> 01:46:37,600
I am a little worried about them going too slow.
As I said, I do think we should work to speed  

1100
01:46:37,600 --> 01:46:41,200
the approval process in the FDA.
I do think we should fight against  

1101
01:46:41,200 --> 01:46:46,080
these chatbot bills that you're describing. 
Described individually, I'm against them. I  

1102
01:46:46,080 --> 01:46:51,840
think they're stupid. But I actually think the 
bigger worry is the developing world, where we  

1103
01:46:51,840 --> 01:46:58,160
don't have functioning markets and where we often 
can't build on the technology that we've had. 

1104
01:46:58,160 --> 01:47:01,360
I worry more that those 
folks will get left behind. 

1105
01:47:01,360 --> 01:47:04,960
And I worry that even if the cures are 
developed, maybe there's someone in rural  

1106
01:47:04,960 --> 01:47:10,560
Mississippi who doesn't get it as well.
That's a smaller version of the concern  

1107
01:47:10,560 --> 01:47:14,240
we have in the developing world.
So the things we've been doing  

1108
01:47:14,240 --> 01:47:26,560
are working with philanthropists.
We work with folks who deliver medicine and  

1109
01:47:26,560 --> 01:47:34,160
health interventions to the developing world, 
to sub-Saharan Africa, India, Latin America,  

1110
01:47:34,160 --> 01:47:39,280
and other developing parts of the world.
That's the thing I think that  

1111
01:47:39,280 --> 01:47:42,000
won't happen on its own.
You mentioned export controls.  

1112
01:47:42,960 --> 01:47:47,440
Why shouldn't the US and China both have 
a "country of geniuses in a data center"? 

1113
01:47:48,640 --> 01:47:52,560
Why won’t it happen or why shouldn't it happen?
Why shouldn't it happen. 

1114
01:47:54,720 --> 01:48:02,400
If this does happen, we 
could have a few situations. 

1115
01:48:02,400 --> 01:48:05,360
If we have an offense-dominant 
situation, we could have a situation  

1116
01:48:05,360 --> 01:48:13,360
like nuclear weapons, but more dangerous.
Either side could easily destroy everything. 

1117
01:48:14,000 --> 01:48:19,120
We could also have a world where it's unstable.
The nuclear equilibrium is  

1118
01:48:19,120 --> 01:48:24,160
stable because it's deterrence.
But let's say there was uncertainty about,  

1119
01:48:24,160 --> 01:48:30,240
if the two AIs fought, which AI would win? 
That could create instability. You often have  

1120
01:48:30,240 --> 01:48:34,320
conflict when the two sides have a different 
assessment of their likelihood of winning. 

1121
01:48:34,320 --> 01:48:37,920
If one side is like, "Oh yeah, there's a 90% 
chance I'll win," and the other side thinks  

1122
01:48:40,160 --> 01:48:43,520
the same, then a fight is much more likely.
They can't both be right,  

1123
01:48:43,520 --> 01:48:46,880
but they can both think that.
But this seems like a fully general argument  

1124
01:48:46,880 --> 01:48:52,080
against the diffusion of AI technology.
That's the implication of this world. 

1125
01:48:52,800 --> 01:48:55,920
Let me just go on, because I think 
we will get diffusion eventually. 

1126
01:48:55,920 --> 01:49:00,560
The other concern I have is that governments 
will oppress their own people with AI. 

1127
01:49:04,160 --> 01:49:12,880
I'm worried about a world where you have a country 
in which there’s already a government that's  

1128
01:49:16,240 --> 01:49:21,680
building a high-tech authoritarian state.
To be clear, this is about the government. 

1129
01:49:21,680 --> 01:49:24,400
This is not about the people.
We need to find a way for  

1130
01:49:24,400 --> 01:49:27,760
people everywhere to benefit.
My worry here is about governments. 

1131
01:49:30,080 --> 01:49:33,920
My worry is if the world gets carved up 
into two pieces, one of those two pieces  

1132
01:49:33,920 --> 01:49:38,880
could be authoritarian or totalitarian in 
a way that's very difficult to displace. 

1133
01:49:39,440 --> 01:49:45,440
Now, will governments eventually get powerful 
AI, and is there a risk of authoritarianism?  

1134
01:49:45,440 --> 01:49:48,720
Yes. Will governments eventually get 
powerful AI, and is there a risk of  

1135
01:49:52,080 --> 01:50:00,320
bad equilibria? Yes, I think both things. But the 
initial conditions matter. At some point, we're  

1136
01:50:00,320 --> 01:50:05,440
going to need to set up the rules of the road.
I'm not saying that one country, either the United  

1137
01:50:05,440 --> 01:50:09,760
States or a coalition of democracies—which 
I think would be a better setup, although it  

1138
01:50:09,760 --> 01:50:19,200
requires more international cooperation than we 
currently seem to want to make—should just say,  

1139
01:50:19,200 --> 01:50:22,480
"These are the rules of the road."
There's going to be some negotiation. 

1140
01:50:22,480 --> 01:50:31,040
The world is going to have to grapple with this.
What I would like is for the democratic nations of  

1141
01:50:31,040 --> 01:50:39,280
the world—those whose governments represent 
closer to pro-human values—are holding the  

1142
01:50:39,280 --> 01:50:44,160
stronger hand and have more leverage 
when the rules of the road are set. 

1143
01:50:44,160 --> 01:50:47,120
So I'm very concerned about 
that initial condition. 

1144
01:50:47,120 --> 01:50:51,760
I was re-listening to the interview from 
three years ago, and one of the ways it  

1145
01:50:51,760 --> 01:50:55,920
aged poorly is that I kept asking questions 
assuming there was going to be some key  

1146
01:50:55,920 --> 01:51:00,320
fulcrum moment two to three years from now.
In fact, being that far out, it just seems  

1147
01:51:00,320 --> 01:51:05,840
like progress continues, AI improves, AI is more 
diffused, and people will use it for more things. 

1148
01:51:05,840 --> 01:51:09,840
It seems like you're imagining a world in the 
future where the countries get together, and  

1149
01:51:09,840 --> 01:51:13,600
"Here's the rules of the road, here's the leverage 
we have, and here's the leverage you have." 

1150
01:51:13,600 --> 01:51:17,440
But on the current trajectory, 
everybody will have more AI. 

1151
01:51:18,080 --> 01:51:20,000
Some of that AI will be used 
by authoritarian countries. 

1152
01:51:20,000 --> 01:51:22,400
Some of that within the authoritarian 
countries will be used by private  

1153
01:51:22,400 --> 01:51:26,400
actors versus state actors.
It's not clear who will benefit more. 

1154
01:51:26,400 --> 01:51:30,320
It's always unpredictable to tell in advance.
It seems like the internet privileged  

1155
01:51:30,320 --> 01:51:32,400
authoritarian countries more 
than you would've expected. 

1156
01:51:33,280 --> 01:51:38,800
Maybe AI will be the opposite way around.
I want to better understand what  

1157
01:51:38,800 --> 01:51:42,640
you're imagining here.
Just to be precise about it,  

1158
01:51:42,640 --> 01:51:47,280
I think the exponential of the underlying 
technology will continue as it has before. 

1159
01:51:47,280 --> 01:51:52,480
The models get smarter and smarter, even when they 
get to a "country of geniuses in a data center." 

1160
01:51:53,760 --> 01:51:56,080
I think you can continue 
to make the model smarter. 

1161
01:51:56,080 --> 01:52:01,840
There's a question of getting diminishing 
returns on their value in the world. 

1162
01:52:01,840 --> 01:52:05,840
How much does it matter after 
you've already solved human biology? 

1163
01:52:07,520 --> 01:52:12,720
At some point you can do harder, more abstruse 
math problems, but nothing after that matters. 

1164
01:52:12,720 --> 01:52:18,640
Putting that aside, I do think the exponential 
will continue, but there will be certain  

1165
01:52:18,640 --> 01:52:24,480
distinguished points on the exponential.
Companies, individuals, and countries  

1166
01:52:24,480 --> 01:52:31,600
will reach those points at different times.
In "The Adolescence of Technology" I talk about:  

1167
01:52:31,600 --> 01:52:38,240
Is a nuclear deterrent still 
stable in the world of AI? 

1168
01:52:38,240 --> 01:52:42,560
I don't know, but that's an example 
of one thing we've taken for granted. 

1169
01:52:42,560 --> 01:52:47,680
The technology could reach such a level 
that we can no longer be certain of it.  

1170
01:52:50,160 --> 01:52:57,520
Think of others. There are points where if you 
reach a certain level, maybe you have offensive  

1171
01:52:57,520 --> 01:53:04,080
cyber dominance, and every computer system 
is transparent to you after that unless the  

1172
01:53:04,080 --> 01:53:09,360
other side has an equivalent defense.
I don't know what the critical moment  

1173
01:53:09,360 --> 01:53:14,160
is or if there's a single critical moment.
But I think there will be either a critical  

1174
01:53:14,160 --> 01:53:22,960
moment, a small number of critical moments, 
or some critical window where AI confers  

1175
01:53:22,960 --> 01:53:30,320
some large advantage from the perspective 
of national security, and one country or  

1176
01:53:30,320 --> 01:53:36,640
coalition has reached it before others.
I'm not advocating that they just say,  

1177
01:53:36,640 --> 01:53:40,880
"Okay, we're in charge now."
That's not how I think about it. 

1178
01:53:42,560 --> 01:53:45,440
The other side is always catching up.
There are extreme actions you're not  

1179
01:53:45,440 --> 01:53:52,560
willing to take, and it's not right 
to take complete control anyway. 

1180
01:53:52,560 --> 01:53:58,080
But at the point that happens, people are 
going to understand that the world has changed. 

1181
01:53:58,080 --> 01:54:05,520
There's going to be some negotiation, 
implicit or explicit, about what the  

1182
01:54:05,520 --> 01:54:13,520
post-AI world order looks like.
My interest is in making that  

1183
01:54:14,080 --> 01:54:24,080
negotiation be one in which classical 
liberal democracy has a strong hand. 

1184
01:54:24,080 --> 01:54:26,640
I want to understand what that better 
means, because you say in the essay,  

1185
01:54:27,440 --> 01:54:33,600
"Autocracy is simply not a form of government that 
people can accept in the post-powerful AI age." 

1186
01:54:33,600 --> 01:54:39,920
That sounds like you're saying the CCP as an 
institution cannot exist after we get AGI. 

1187
01:54:41,040 --> 01:54:47,920
That seems like a very strong demand, and it 
seems to imply a world where the leading lab  

1188
01:54:47,920 --> 01:54:54,240
or the leading country will be able to—and 
by that language, should get to—determine  

1189
01:54:54,240 --> 01:55:00,000
how the world is governed or what kinds 
of governments are, and are not, allowed. 

1190
01:55:02,400 --> 01:55:09,520
I believe that paragraph said something like, 
"You could take it even further and say X." 

1191
01:55:13,360 --> 01:55:15,760
I wasn't necessarily endorsing that view.
I was saying,  

1192
01:55:18,080 --> 01:55:24,240
"Here's a weaker thing that I believe.
We have to worry a lot about authoritarians and  

1193
01:55:24,240 --> 01:55:30,400
we should try to check them and limit their power.
You could take this much further and have a more  

1194
01:55:30,400 --> 01:55:38,240
interventionist view that says authoritarian 
countries with AI are these self-fulfilling  

1195
01:55:38,240 --> 01:55:43,920
cycles that are very hard to displace, so you 
just need to get rid of them from the beginning." 

1196
01:55:43,920 --> 01:55:49,120
That has exactly all the problems you say.
If you were to make a commitment to  

1197
01:55:49,120 --> 01:55:53,440
overthrowing every authoritarian country, 
they would take a bunch of actions now  

1198
01:55:53,440 --> 01:56:02,240
that could lead to instability.
That just may not be possible. 

1199
01:56:02,240 --> 01:56:09,120
But the point I was making that I do 
endorse is that it is quite possible that... 

1200
01:56:09,120 --> 01:56:16,320
Today, the view, my view, in most of the Western 
world is that democracy is a better form of  

1201
01:56:16,320 --> 01:56:21,040
government than authoritarianism.
But if a country’s authoritarian,  

1202
01:56:21,040 --> 01:56:25,280
we don’t react the way we’d react if 
they committed a genocide or something. 

1203
01:56:27,360 --> 01:56:32,560
I guess what I'm saying is I'm a little worried 
that in the age of AGI, authoritarianism will  

1204
01:56:32,560 --> 01:56:34,960
have a different meaning.
It will be a graver thing. 

1205
01:56:35,920 --> 01:56:39,920
We have to decide one way or 
another how to deal with that. 

1206
01:56:39,920 --> 01:56:47,680
The interventionist view is one possible view. I 
was exploring such views. It may end up being the  

1207
01:56:47,680 --> 01:56:55,280
right view, or it may end up being too extreme. 
But I do have hope. One piece of hope I have is  

1208
01:56:55,280 --> 01:57:04,480
that we have seen that as new technologies are 
invented, forms of government become obsolete. 

1209
01:57:04,480 --> 01:57:10,640
I mentioned this in "Adolescence of 
Technology", where I said feudalism  

1210
01:57:10,640 --> 01:57:18,800
was basically a form of government, and when 
we invented industrialization, feudalism was no  

1211
01:57:18,800 --> 01:57:23,120
longer sustainable. It no longer made sense.
Why is that hope? Couldn't that imply that  

1212
01:57:23,120 --> 01:57:26,240
democracy is no longer going 
to be a competitive system? 

1213
01:57:26,880 --> 01:57:38,160
Right, it could go either way.
But these problems with  

1214
01:57:38,160 --> 01:57:44,320
authoritarianism get deeper.
I wonder if that's an indicator of  

1215
01:57:44,320 --> 01:57:52,480
other problems that authoritarianism will have.
In other words, because authoritarianism becomes  

1216
01:57:52,480 --> 01:57:58,160
worse, people are more afraid of it.
They work harder to stop it. 

1217
01:57:59,440 --> 01:58:07,280
You have to think in terms of total equilibrium.
I just wonder if it will motivate new ways  

1218
01:58:07,280 --> 01:58:13,680
of thinking about how to preserve and 
protect freedom with the new technology. 

1219
01:58:13,680 --> 01:58:22,240
Even more optimistically, will it lead to 
a collective reckoning and a more emphatic  

1220
01:58:22,240 --> 01:58:26,720
realization of how important some of the 
things we take as individual rights are? 

1221
01:58:27,360 --> 01:58:32,720
A more emphatic realization that 
we really can't give these away. 

1222
01:58:32,720 --> 01:58:36,160
We've seen there's no other way 
to live that actually works. 

1223
01:58:39,520 --> 01:58:46,560
I am actually hopeful that—it sounds too 
idealistic, but I believe it could be the  

1224
01:58:46,560 --> 01:58:52,960
case—dictatorships become morally obsolete.
They become morally unworkable forms of  

1225
01:58:52,960 --> 01:59:02,080
government and the crisis that that creates 
is sufficient to force us to find another way. 

1226
01:59:03,680 --> 01:59:07,120
I think there is genuinely a tough question 
here which I'm not sure how you resolve. 

1227
01:59:07,920 --> 01:59:11,040
We've had to come out one way or 
another on it through history. 

1228
01:59:11,760 --> 01:59:15,360
With China in the '70s and '80s, 
we decided that even though it's an  

1229
01:59:15,360 --> 01:59:18,960
authoritarian system, we will engage with it.
I think in retrospect that was the right call,  

1230
01:59:18,960 --> 01:59:23,280
because it’s a state authoritarian system but 
a billion-plus people are much wealthier and  

1231
01:59:23,280 --> 01:59:27,280
better off than they would've otherwise been.
It's not clear that it would've stopped being  

1232
01:59:27,280 --> 01:59:30,480
an authoritarian country otherwise.
You can just look at North Korea  

1233
01:59:30,480 --> 01:59:33,760
as an example of that.
I don't know if it takes  

1234
01:59:34,400 --> 01:59:40,800
that much intelligence to remain an authoritarian 
country that continues to coalesce its own power. 

1235
01:59:40,800 --> 01:59:44,560
You can imagine a North Korea with an AI 
that's much worse than everybody else's,  

1236
01:59:44,560 --> 01:59:50,000
but still enough to keep power.
In general, it seems like we should just  

1237
01:59:50,000 --> 01:59:54,720
have this attitude that the benefits of 
AI—in the form of all these empowerments  

1238
01:59:54,720 --> 02:00:00,320
of humanity and health—will be big.
Historically, we have decided it's good  

1239
02:00:00,320 --> 02:00:05,360
to spread the benefits of technology widely, even 
to people whose governments are authoritarian. 

1240
02:00:06,000 --> 02:00:10,400
It is a tough question, how to think about it 
with AI, but historically we have said, "yes,  

1241
02:00:10,400 --> 02:00:14,480
this is a positive-sum world, and it's 
still worth diffusing the technology." 

1242
02:00:15,280 --> 02:00:20,880
There are a number of choices we have.
Framing this as a government-to-government  

1243
02:00:20,880 --> 02:00:27,840
decision in national security terms is one 
lens, but there are a lot of other lenses. 

1244
02:00:27,840 --> 02:00:31,520
You could imagine a world where we 
produce all these cures to diseases. 

1245
02:00:32,400 --> 02:00:38,160
The cures are fine to sell to authoritarian 
countries, but the data centers just aren't. 

1246
02:00:38,160 --> 02:00:44,400
The chips and the data centers aren't, 
and the AI industry itself isn't. 

1247
02:00:44,400 --> 02:00:48,320
Another possibility I think 
folks should think about is this. 

1248
02:00:49,360 --> 02:00:55,280
Could there be developments we can make—either 
that naturally happen as a result of AI,  

1249
02:00:55,280 --> 02:00:59,840
or that we could make happen by 
building technology on AI—that  

1250
02:00:59,840 --> 02:01:05,360
create an equilibrium where it becomes 
infeasible for authoritarian countries  

1251
02:01:05,360 --> 02:01:09,600
to deny their people private use 
of the benefits of the technology? 

1252
02:01:12,400 --> 02:01:18,080
Are there equilibria where we can give everyone in 
an authoritarian country their own AI model that  

1253
02:01:19,600 --> 02:01:24,880
defends them from surveillance and there isn't 
a way for the authoritarian country to crack  

1254
02:01:24,880 --> 02:01:29,840
down on this while retaining power? I don't know. 
That sounds to me like if that went far enough,  

1255
02:01:29,840 --> 02:01:35,040
it would be a reason why authoritarian 
countries would disintegrate from the inside. 

1256
02:01:35,040 --> 02:01:39,280
But maybe there's a middle world where there's 
an equilibrium where, if they want to hold on  

1257
02:01:39,280 --> 02:01:45,440
to power, the authoritarians can't deny 
individualized access to the technology. 

1258
02:01:45,440 --> 02:01:49,680
But I actually do have a hope 
for the more radical version. 

1259
02:01:50,880 --> 02:01:54,880
Is it possible that the technology 
might inherently have properties—or  

1260
02:01:54,880 --> 02:02:01,200
that by building on it in certain ways 
we could create properties—that have this  

1261
02:02:01,200 --> 02:02:07,280
dissolving effect on authoritarian structures?
Now, we hoped originally—think back to the  

1262
02:02:07,280 --> 02:02:13,200
beginning of the Obama administration—that 
social media and the internet would have  

1263
02:02:13,200 --> 02:02:20,080
that property, and it turns out not to.
But what if we could try again with the  

1264
02:02:20,080 --> 02:02:23,680
knowledge of how many things could go wrong, 
and that this is a different technology? 

1265
02:02:23,680 --> 02:02:25,680
I don't know if it would 
work, but it's worth a try. 

1266
02:02:26,480 --> 02:02:30,160
It's just very unpredictable. There 
are first principles reasons why  

1267
02:02:30,160 --> 02:02:35,280
authoritarianism might be privileged.
It's all very unpredictable. We just  

1268
02:02:35,280 --> 02:02:40,880
have to recognize the problem and come 
up with 10 things we can try, try those,  

1269
02:02:40,880 --> 02:02:46,373
and then assess which ones are working, if any.
Then try new ones if the old ones aren't working. 

1270
02:02:46,373 --> 02:02:51,440
But I guess that nets out to today, as you 
say, that we will not sell data centers,  

1271
02:02:51,440 --> 02:02:58,240
or chips, and the ability to make chips to China.
So in some sense, you are denying… There would be  

1272
02:02:58,240 --> 02:03:02,480
some benefits to the Chinese economy, Chinese 
people, et cetera, because we're doing that. 

1273
02:03:02,480 --> 02:03:06,080
Then there'd also be benefits to the American 
economy because it's a positive-sum world.  

1274
02:03:06,080 --> 02:03:08,720
We could trade. They could have their 
country's data centers doing one thing. 

1275
02:03:08,720 --> 02:03:14,800
We could have ours doing another.
Already, you're saying it's not worth that  

1276
02:03:14,800 --> 02:03:22,720
positive-sum stipend to empower those countries?
What I would say is that we are about to be  

1277
02:03:22,720 --> 02:03:27,840
in a world where growth and economic 
value will come very easily if we're  

1278
02:03:27,840 --> 02:03:35,040
able to build these powerful AI models.
What will not come easily is distribution  

1279
02:03:35,040 --> 02:03:39,680
of benefits, distribution of 
wealth, political freedom. 

1280
02:03:40,720 --> 02:03:43,200
These are the things that are 
going to be hard to achieve. 

1281
02:03:43,200 --> 02:03:50,320
So when I think about policy, I think that the 
technology and the market will deliver all the  

1282
02:03:50,320 --> 02:03:54,400
fundamental benefits, this is my fundamental 
belief, almost faster than we can take them. 

1283
02:03:55,840 --> 02:04:02,800
These questions about distribution and political 
freedom and rights are the ones that will actually  

1284
02:04:02,800 --> 02:04:06,400
matter and that policy should focus on.
Speaking of distribution, as you were  

1285
02:04:06,400 --> 02:04:12,880
mentioning, we have developing countries.
In many cases, catch-up growth has been  

1286
02:04:12,880 --> 02:04:15,440
weaker than we would have hoped for.
But when catch-up growth does happen,  

1287
02:04:15,440 --> 02:04:18,480
it's fundamentally because 
they have underutilized labor. 

1288
02:04:18,480 --> 02:04:21,280
We can bring the capital and know-how from 
developed countries to these countries,  

1289
02:04:21,280 --> 02:04:26,640
and then they can grow quite rapidly.
Obviously, in a world where labor is no  

1290
02:04:26,640 --> 02:04:30,160
longer the constraining factor, 
this mechanism no longer works. 

1291
02:04:30,160 --> 02:04:33,600
So is the hope basically to 
rely on philanthropy from  

1292
02:04:33,600 --> 02:04:37,840
the people or countries who immediately 
get wealthy from AI? What is the hope? 

1293
02:04:38,880 --> 02:04:44,400
Philanthropy should obviously play 
some role, as it has in the past. 

1294
02:04:44,400 --> 02:04:49,920
But I think growth is always better and 
stronger if we can make it endogenous. 

1295
02:04:50,640 --> 02:04:54,720
What are the relevant industries 
in an AI-driven world? 

1296
02:04:58,080 --> 02:05:00,880
I said we shouldn't build data centers in 
China, but there's no reason we shouldn't  

1297
02:05:00,880 --> 02:05:04,080
build data centers in Africa.
In fact, I think it'd be  

1298
02:05:04,080 --> 02:05:07,920
great to build data centers in Africa.
As long as they're not owned by China,  

1299
02:05:08,960 --> 02:05:13,600
we should build data centers in Africa.
I think that's a great thing to do. 

1300
02:05:16,320 --> 02:05:21,360
There's no reason we can't build a 
pharmaceutical industry that's AI-driven. 

1301
02:05:22,960 --> 02:05:28,880
If AI is accelerating drug discovery, then 
there will be a bunch of biotech startups. 

1302
02:05:28,880 --> 02:05:31,280
Let's make sure some of those 
happen in the developing world. 

1303
02:05:31,280 --> 02:05:34,960
Certainly, during the transition—we can 
talk about the point where humans have no  

1304
02:05:34,960 --> 02:05:41,120
role—humans will still have some role in starting 
up these companies and supervising the AI models. 

1305
02:05:41,120 --> 02:05:44,160
So let's make sure some of those 
humans are in the developing world  

1306
02:05:44,160 --> 02:05:48,240
so that fast growth can happen there as well.
You guys recently announced that Claude is going  

1307
02:05:48,240 --> 02:05:53,120
to have a constitution that's aligned to a set of 
values, and not necessarily just to the end user. 

1308
02:05:53,120 --> 02:05:56,080
There's a world I can imagine where 
if it is aligned to the end user,  

1309
02:05:56,080 --> 02:05:59,600
it preserves the balance of power we have in the 
world today because everybody gets to have their  

1310
02:05:59,600 --> 02:06:03,680
own AI that's advocating for them.
The ratio of bad actors to  

1311
02:06:03,680 --> 02:06:06,240
good actors stays constant.
It seems to work out for our world today. 

1312
02:06:07,360 --> 02:06:12,880
Why is it better not to do that, but to 
have a specific set of values that the  

1313
02:06:12,880 --> 02:06:16,720
AI should carry forward?
I'm not sure I'd quite  

1314
02:06:16,720 --> 02:06:21,600
draw the distinction in that way.
There may be two relevant distinctions here. 

1315
02:06:22,400 --> 02:06:27,520
I think you're talking about a mix of the two.
One is, should we give the model a set of  

1316
02:06:27,520 --> 02:06:31,520
instructions about "do this" 
versus "don't do this"? 

1317
02:06:31,520 --> 02:06:37,440
The other is, should we give the model 
a set of principles for how to act? 

1318
02:06:44,080 --> 02:06:48,880
It's kind of purely a practical and 
empirical thing that we've observed. 

1319
02:06:48,880 --> 02:06:52,960
By teaching the model principles, 
getting it to learn from principles,  

1320
02:06:52,960 --> 02:06:58,160
its behavior is more consistent, it's easier 
to cover edge cases, and the model is more  

1321
02:06:58,160 --> 02:07:09,600
likely to do what people want it to do.
In other words, if you give it a list of  

1322
02:07:09,600 --> 02:07:10,800
rules—"don't tell people how to hot-wire 
a car, don't speak in Korean"—it doesn't  

1323
02:07:10,800 --> 02:07:14,320
really understand the rules, and 
it's hard to generalize from them. 

1324
02:07:15,440 --> 02:07:20,720
It’s just a list of do’s and don’t’s.
Whereas if you give it principles—it  

1325
02:07:21,440 --> 02:07:25,360
has some hard guardrails like "Don't make 
biological weapons" but—overall you're  

1326
02:07:25,360 --> 02:07:31,120
trying to understand what it should be aiming 
to do, how it should be aiming to operate. 

1327
02:07:31,120 --> 02:07:35,920
So just from a practical perspective, that turns 
out to be a more effective way to train the model. 

1328
02:07:35,920 --> 02:07:42,240
That's the rules versus principles trade-off.
Then there's another thing you're talking about,  

1329
02:07:42,240 --> 02:07:50,960
which is the corrigibility versus 
intrinsic motivation trade-off. 

1330
02:07:51,680 --> 02:08:02,000
How much should the model be a kind 
of "skin suit" where it just directly  

1331
02:08:02,000 --> 02:08:05,840
follows the instructions given to it by 
whoever is giving those instructions,  

1332
02:08:06,560 --> 02:08:12,240
versus how much should the model have an inherent 
set of values and go off and do things on its own? 

1333
02:08:14,320 --> 02:08:20,480
There I would actually say everything about 
the model is closer to the direction that  

1334
02:08:21,760 --> 02:08:24,560
it should mostly do what people want. 
It should mostly follow instructions.  

1335
02:08:24,560 --> 02:08:29,360
We're not trying to build something that 
goes off and runs the world on its own. 

1336
02:08:29,360 --> 02:08:34,240
We're actually pretty far on the corrigible side.
Now, what we do say is there are certain  

1337
02:08:34,240 --> 02:08:40,560
things that the model won't do.
I think we say it in various ways in the  

1338
02:08:40,560 --> 02:08:45,280
constitution, that under normal circumstances, if 
someone asks the model to do a task, it should do  

1339
02:08:45,280 --> 02:08:51,280
that task. That should be the default. But if 
you've asked it to do something dangerous, or  

1340
02:08:54,320 --> 02:09:01,280
to harm someone else, then the 
model is unwilling to do that. 

1341
02:09:01,280 --> 02:09:07,680
So I actually think of it as a mostly 
corrigible model that has some limits,  

1342
02:09:07,680 --> 02:09:12,080
but those limits are based on principles.
Then the fundamental question is,  

1343
02:09:12,080 --> 02:09:15,600
how are those principles determined?
This is not a special question for Anthropic. 

1344
02:09:15,600 --> 02:09:22,160
This would be a question for any AI company.
But because you have been the ones to actually  

1345
02:09:22,160 --> 02:09:25,360
write down the principles, I 
get to ask you this question. 

1346
02:09:25,360 --> 02:09:29,520
Normally, a constitution is written down, 
set in stone, and there's a process of  

1347
02:09:29,520 --> 02:09:34,560
updating it and changing it and so forth.
In this case, it seems like a document  

1348
02:09:34,560 --> 02:09:37,680
that people at Anthropic write, 
that can be changed at any time,  

1349
02:09:37,680 --> 02:09:44,480
that guides the behavior of systems that are going 
to be the basis of a lot of economic activity. 

1350
02:09:45,680 --> 02:09:49,600
How do you think about how 
those principles should be set? 

1351
02:09:50,880 --> 02:09:58,160
I think there are maybe three sizes 
of loop here, three ways to iterate. 

1352
02:09:58,160 --> 02:10:01,920
One is we iterate within Anthropic.
We train the model, we're not happy with it,  

1353
02:10:01,920 --> 02:10:05,600
and we change the constitution.
I think that's good to do. 

1354
02:10:06,480 --> 02:10:10,608
Putting out public updates to the 
constitution every once in a while  

1355
02:10:10,608 --> 02:10:16,640
is good because people can comment on it.
The second level of loop is different companies  

1356
02:10:16,640 --> 02:10:21,513
having different constitutions. I think it’s 
useful. Anthropic puts out a constitution,  

1357
02:10:21,513 --> 02:10:28,800
Gemini puts out a constitution, and 
other companies put out a constitution. 

1358
02:10:28,800 --> 02:10:34,160
People can look at them and compare.
Outside observers can critique and say,  

1359
02:10:34,800 --> 02:10:39,200
"I like this thing from this constitution 
and this thing from that constitution." 

1360
02:10:40,160 --> 02:10:45,760
That creates a soft incentive and 
feedback for all the companies to  

1361
02:10:45,760 --> 02:10:50,400
take the best of each element and improve.
Then I think there's a third loop, which is  

1362
02:10:50,400 --> 02:10:58,720
society beyond the AI companies and beyond 
just those who comment without hard power.  

1363
02:10:59,280 --> 02:11:04,720
There we've done some experiments. A couple years 
ago, we did an experiment with the Collective  

1364
02:11:04,720 --> 02:11:12,320
Intelligence Project to basically poll people and 
ask them what should be in our AI constitution. 

1365
02:11:15,200 --> 02:11:17,360
At the time, we incorporated 
some of those changes. 

1366
02:11:17,360 --> 02:11:19,600
So you could imagine doing something 
like that with the new approach we've  

1367
02:11:19,600 --> 02:11:23,840
taken to the constitution.
It's a little harder because  

1368
02:11:23,840 --> 02:11:28,560
it was an easier approach to take when the 
constitution was a list of dos and don'ts. 

1369
02:11:29,120 --> 02:11:32,880
At the level of principles, it has to 
have a certain amount of coherence. 

1370
02:11:32,880 --> 02:11:37,920
But you could still imagine getting 
views from a wide variety of people. 

1371
02:11:37,920 --> 02:11:42,080
You could also imagine—and this 
is a crazy idea, but this whole  

1372
02:11:42,080 --> 02:11:50,560
interview is about crazy ideas—systems of 
representative government having input. 

1373
02:11:52,320 --> 02:11:55,520
I wouldn't do this today because 
the legislative process is so slow. 

1374
02:11:55,520 --> 02:12:00,080
This is exactly why I think we should be careful 
about the legislative process and AI regulation. 

1375
02:12:00,080 --> 02:12:06,960
But there's no reason you couldn't, in principle, 
say, "All AI models have to have a constitution  

1376
02:12:06,960 --> 02:12:13,360
that starts with these things, and then you can 
append other things after it, but there has to  

1377
02:12:13,360 --> 02:12:18,960
be this special section that takes precedence." 
I wouldn't do that. That's too rigid and sounds  

1378
02:12:22,160 --> 02:12:26,320
overly prescriptive in a way that I 
think overly aggressive legislation is. 

1379
02:12:26,320 --> 02:12:32,720
But that is a thing you could try to do.
Is there some much less heavy-handed  

1380
02:12:32,720 --> 02:12:36,240
version of that? Maybe.
I really like control loop two. 

1381
02:12:37,840 --> 02:12:41,760
Obviously, this is not how constitutions 
of actual governments do or should work. 

1382
02:12:42,480 --> 02:12:46,960
There's not this vague sense in which the 
Supreme Court will feel out how people  

1383
02:12:46,960 --> 02:12:50,000
are feeling—what are the vibes—and 
update the constitution accordingly. 

1384
02:12:50,800 --> 02:12:54,480
With actual governments, there's 
a more formal, procedural process. 

1385
02:12:55,040 --> 02:13:01,440
But you have a vision of competition between 
constitutions, which is actually very reminiscent  

1386
02:13:01,440 --> 02:13:07,440
of how some libertarian charter cities people used 
to talk, about what an archipelago of different  

1387
02:13:07,440 --> 02:13:10,480
kinds of governments would look like.
There would be selection among them of  

1388
02:13:10,480 --> 02:13:14,480
who could operate the most effectively 
and where people would be the happiest. 

1389
02:13:15,200 --> 02:13:21,840
In a sense, you're recreating that 
vision of a utopia of archipelagos. 

1390
02:13:23,360 --> 02:13:30,000
I think that vision has things to recommend 
it and things that will go wrong with it. 

1391
02:13:31,760 --> 02:13:34,720
It's an interesting, in some ways 
compelling, vision, but things will  

1392
02:13:34,720 --> 02:13:40,560
go wrong that you hadn't imagined.
So I like loop two as well,  

1393
02:13:40,560 --> 02:13:46,400
but I feel like the whole thing has got to 
be some mix of loops one, two, and three,  

1394
02:13:46,400 --> 02:13:51,600
and it's a matter of the proportions.
I think that's gotta be the answer. 

1395
02:13:53,040 --> 02:13:58,960
When somebody eventually writes the equivalent 
of The Making of the Atomic Bomb for this era,  

1396
02:13:58,960 --> 02:14:02,560
what is the thing that will be hardest 
to glean from the historical record that  

1397
02:14:02,560 --> 02:14:06,960
they're most likely to miss?
I think a few things. One is,  

1398
02:14:06,960 --> 02:14:12,560
at every moment of this exponential, the extent to 
which the world outside it didn't understand it. 

1399
02:14:12,560 --> 02:14:17,360
This is a bias that's often present in history.
Anything that actually happened looks  

1400
02:14:17,360 --> 02:14:24,240
inevitable in retrospect.
When people look back, it will  

1401
02:14:24,240 --> 02:14:32,080
be hard for them to put themselves in the place 
of people who were actually making a bet on this  

1402
02:14:32,080 --> 02:14:38,320
thing to happen that wasn't inevitable, that we 
had these arguments like the arguments I make for  

1403
02:14:38,320 --> 02:14:48,800
scaling or that continual learning will be solved.
Some of us internally put a high probability  

1404
02:14:48,800 --> 02:14:56,560
on this happening, but there's a world 
outside us that's not acting on that at all. 

1405
02:14:58,960 --> 02:15:04,960
I think the weirdness of it, 
unfortunately the insularity of it... 

1406
02:15:07,520 --> 02:15:10,240
If we're one year or two 
years away from it happening,  

1407
02:15:10,240 --> 02:15:14,160
the average person on the street has no idea.
That's one of the things I'm trying to change with  

1408
02:15:14,160 --> 02:15:19,040
the memos, with talking to policymakers.
I don’t know but I think  

1409
02:15:19,040 --> 02:15:27,280
that's just a crazy thing.
Finally, I would say—and this  

1410
02:15:27,280 --> 02:15:33,920
probably applies to almost all historical moments 
of crisis—how absolutely fast it was happening,  

1411
02:15:33,920 --> 02:15:39,600
how everything was happening all at once.
Decisions that you might think were  

1412
02:15:39,600 --> 02:15:42,720
carefully calculated, well actually 
you have to make that decision,  

1413
02:15:42,720 --> 02:15:47,840
and then you have to make 30 other decisions on 
the same day because it's all happening so fast. 

1414
02:15:47,840 --> 02:15:50,960
You don't even know which decisions are 
going to turn out to be consequential. 

1415
02:15:52,320 --> 02:16:00,880
One of my worries—although it's also an 
insight into what's happening—is that some  

1416
02:16:00,880 --> 02:16:05,920
very critical decision will be some decision 
where someone just comes into my office and  

1417
02:16:05,920 --> 02:16:13,120
is like, "Dario, you have two minutes.
Should we do thing A or thing B on this?" 

1418
02:16:14,640 --> 02:16:20,720
Someone gives me this random half-page memo 
and asks, "Should we do A or B?" I'm like, "I  

1419
02:16:20,720 --> 02:16:26,080
don't know. I have to eat lunch. Let's do B." That 
ends up being the most consequential thing ever. 

1420
02:16:26,080 --> 02:16:35,520
So final question. There aren't tech CEOs who are 
usually writing 50-page memos every few months. 

1421
02:16:35,520 --> 02:16:40,081
It seems like you have managed to build 
a role for yourself and a company around  

1422
02:16:40,080 --> 02:16:47,280
you which is compatible with this 
more intellectual-type role of CEO. 

1423
02:16:47,280 --> 02:16:53,759
I want to understand how you construct that. 
How does that work? Do you just go away for  

1424
02:16:53,760 --> 02:16:56,240
a couple of weeks and then you tell your 
company, "This is the memo. Here's what  

1425
02:16:56,240 --> 02:16:58,800
we're doing"? It's also reported that 
you write a bunch of these internally. 

1426
02:16:59,680 --> 02:17:02,160
For this particular one, I 
wrote it over winter break. 

1427
02:17:04,319 --> 02:17:08,639
I was having a hard time finding 
the time to actually write it. 

1428
02:17:08,640 --> 02:17:13,760
But I think about this in a broader way.
I think it relates to the culture of the company. 

1429
02:17:13,760 --> 02:17:19,040
I probably spend a third, maybe 40%, of my time 
making sure the culture of Anthropic is good. 

1430
02:17:19,040 --> 02:17:26,960
As Anthropic has gotten larger, it's gotten 
harder to get directly involved in the training  

1431
02:17:26,960 --> 02:17:30,880
of the models, the launch of the models, 
the building of the products. It's 2,500  

1432
02:17:30,880 --> 02:17:39,680
people. I have certain instincts, but it's very 
difficult to get involved in every single detail. 

1433
02:17:41,120 --> 02:17:46,960
I try as much as possible, but one thing that's 
very leveraged is making sure Anthropic is a good  

1434
02:17:46,960 --> 02:17:51,920
place to work, people like working there, everyone 
thinks of themselves as team members, and everyone  

1435
02:17:51,920 --> 02:17:57,280
works together instead of against each other.
We've seen as some of the other AI companies  

1436
02:17:57,280 --> 02:18:03,200
have grown—without naming any names—we're starting 
to see decoherence and people fighting each other. 

1437
02:18:03,200 --> 02:18:07,359
I would argue there was even a lot of that 
from the beginning, but it's gotten worse. 

1438
02:18:08,160 --> 02:18:14,720
I think we've done an extraordinarily good 
job, even if not perfect, of holding the  

1439
02:18:14,720 --> 02:18:19,920
company together, making everyone feel the 
mission, that we're sincere about the mission,  

1440
02:18:19,920 --> 02:18:23,920
and that everyone has faith that everyone 
else there is working for the right reason. 

1441
02:18:23,920 --> 02:18:28,080
That we're a team, that people aren't trying 
to get ahead at each other's expense or  

1442
02:18:28,080 --> 02:18:32,000
backstab each other, which again, I think 
happens a lot at some of the other places. 

1443
02:18:33,200 --> 02:18:36,559
How do you make that the case? 
It's a lot of things. It's me,  

1444
02:18:36,560 --> 02:18:41,121
it's Daniela, who runs the company 
day to day, it's the co-founders,  

1445
02:18:41,120 --> 02:18:44,399
it's the other people we hire, it's 
the environment we try to create. 

1446
02:18:44,399 --> 02:18:53,839
But I think an important thing in the culture is 
that the other leaders as well, but especially me,  

1447
02:18:53,840 --> 02:18:58,880
have to articulate what the company is 
about, why it's doing what it's doing,  

1448
02:18:58,880 --> 02:19:04,160
what its strategy is, what its values are, 
what its mission is, and what it stands for. 

1449
02:19:06,319 --> 02:19:09,920
When you get to 2,500 people, you 
can't do that person by person. 

1450
02:19:09,920 --> 02:19:12,560
You have to write, or you have 
to speak to the whole company. 

1451
02:19:12,560 --> 02:19:17,201
This is why I get up in front of the whole 
company every two weeks and speak for an hour. 

1452
02:19:18,319 --> 02:19:22,399
I wouldn't say I write essays internally. 
I do two things. One, I write this thing  

1453
02:19:22,399 --> 02:19:27,359
called a DVQ, Dario Vision Quest.
I wasn't the one who named it that. 

1454
02:19:27,359 --> 02:19:32,080
That's the name it received, and it's one of these 
names that I tried to fight because it made it  

1455
02:19:32,080 --> 02:19:38,080
sound like I was going off and smoking peyote or 
something. But the name just stuck. So I get up  

1456
02:19:38,080 --> 02:19:43,040
in front of the company every two weeks.
I have a three or four-page document,  

1457
02:19:43,040 --> 02:19:48,399
and I just talk through three or four different 
topics about what's going on internally,  

1458
02:19:49,040 --> 02:19:54,080
the models we're producing, the products, 
the outside industry, the world as a whole  

1459
02:19:54,080 --> 02:19:59,840
as it relates to AI and geopolitically 
in general. Just some mix of that. I go  

1460
02:19:59,840 --> 02:20:06,880
through very honestly and I say, "This is what I'm 
thinking, and this is what Anthropic leadership  

1461
02:20:06,880 --> 02:20:13,600
is thinking," and then I answer questions.
That direct connection has a lot of value that  

1462
02:20:13,600 --> 02:20:18,241
is hard to achieve when you're passing 
things down the chain six levels deep. 

1463
02:20:19,040 --> 02:20:26,640
A large fraction of the company comes to 
attend, either in person or virtually. 

1464
02:20:27,359 --> 02:20:32,880
It really means that you can communicate a lot.
The other thing I do is I have a channel in  

1465
02:20:32,880 --> 02:20:35,920
Slack where I just write a bunch 
of things and comment a lot. 

1466
02:20:36,720 --> 02:20:42,480
Often that's in response to things I'm seeing 
at the company or questions people ask. 

1467
02:20:44,319 --> 02:20:49,120
We do internal surveys and there are things people 
are concerned about, and so I'll write them up. 

1468
02:20:50,640 --> 02:20:56,161
I'm just very honest about these things.
I just say them very directly. 

1469
02:20:56,160 --> 02:21:01,519
The point is to get a reputation of telling the 
company the truth about what's happening, to call  

1470
02:21:01,520 --> 02:21:07,680
things what they are, to acknowledge problems, 
to avoid the sort of corpo speak, the kind of  

1471
02:21:07,680 --> 02:21:14,480
defensive communication that often is necessary in 
public because the world is very large and full of  

1472
02:21:14,479 --> 02:21:21,839
people who are interpreting things in bad faith.
But if you have a company of people who you trust,  

1473
02:21:21,840 --> 02:21:29,680
and we try to hire people that we trust, then 
you can really just be entirely unfiltered. 

1474
02:21:31,600 --> 02:21:33,600
I think that's an enormous 
strength of the company. 

1475
02:21:33,600 --> 02:21:38,080
It makes it a better place to work, it makes 
people more than the sum of their parts,  

1476
02:21:38,080 --> 02:21:41,519
and increases the likelihood that we accomplish 
the mission because everyone is on the same page  

1477
02:21:41,520 --> 02:21:46,161
about the mission, and everyone is debating and 
discussing how best to accomplish the mission. 

1478
02:21:46,160 --> 02:21:50,000
Well, in lieu of an external Dario 
Vision Quest, we have this interview. 

1479
02:21:50,000 --> 02:21:54,640
This interview is a little like that.
This has been fun, Dario. Thanks for doing it. 

1480
02:21:54,640 --> 02:22:00,960
Thank you, Dwarkesh.

